// GENERATED by content/build_certs.mjs from content/certs/*.json. Do not edit by hand.
const CERTS = [{"id":"dbx-dea","vendor":"Databricks","name":"Databricks Certified Data Engineer Associate","short":"Databricks DE Associate","fee":"US$200 plus tax","minutes":90,"scored":45,"valid":"2 years","guide":"Exam published 4 May 2026, exam guide updated 6 Aug 2026","guideUrl":"https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Data-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf","disclaimer":"Independent practice written by Datakul from the public exam guide. Not affiliated with or endorsed by Databricks, and not real exam questions.","domains":[{"id":"platform","name":"Databricks Intelligence Platform","w":6,"study":{"cert":"dbx-dea","dom":"platform","intro":"The smallest section, 6% of the exam, but every other section builds on it: how Databricks is laid out, what Delta Lake and Unity Catalog do, and which compute to pick for a job. Expect scenario questions that ask for the best compute for a workload. Work the solved problems with the answer hidden, then practise.","expects":["Describe the platform's main parts: the control plane, the compute plane, Delta Lake for storage and Unity Catalog for governance.","Compare the compute options (serverless, classic jobs and all-purpose compute, SQL warehouses), their limits and how they are charged.","Choose the right compute for a workload."],"ideas":[{"t":"Two planes","b":"The control plane is Databricks' side: the web app, job scheduler, Unity Catalog and other services. The compute plane is where your code runs. Serverless compute runs in Databricks' own account (in your workspace's region); classic compute runs as virtual machines in your own cloud account and network. Your tables and files stay in cloud object storage either way."},{"t":"Delta Lake: tables on files","b":"A Delta table is Parquet data files plus a transaction log (_delta_log). Every change is a commit to the log, which gives ACID transactions, time travel to earlier versions, schema enforcement, and MERGE, UPDATE and DELETE on files that are otherwise write-once. Delta is the default table format on Databricks."},{"t":"Unity Catalog: one place for access and lineage","b":"Unity Catalog holds the metastore: catalogs, schemas, tables, views, volumes, functions and models, named catalog.schema.object. It controls who can use each object, records lineage and audit logs, and is shared by every workspace attached to the metastore. Volumes govern files the same way tables govern rows."},{"t":"Serverless first","b":"Databricks recommends serverless for notebooks, jobs and pipelines when it supports what you need. It starts in seconds, scales on its own, needs no cluster settings, and you pay only while it works, with no idle machines. It does not support R or RDD APIs, and you cannot set executor memory or most Spark settings."},{"t":"Classic compute","b":"Jobs compute is created for a job run and shut down after it, and is charged at a lower rate than all-purpose compute. All-purpose compute is for interactive work, shared by people, and costs money while idle until auto termination stops it. Use classic when you need R, RDD APIs, custom Spark settings, specific instance types, GPUs or init scripts. Access mode Standard (formerly shared) is for several users; Dedicated (formerly single user) is for one user or group and supports R and ML runtimes."},{"t":"SQL warehouses","b":"SQL warehouses run SQL for queries, dashboards, BI tools and SQL tasks. Serverless starts in about 2 to 6 seconds and has intelligent workload management; Pro and Classic run in your account and start in about 4 minutes. Pro has Predictive IO; Classic is entry level. Choose Pro when serverless is not available in your region or you need custom networking."},{"t":"How it is charged","b":"Databricks charges in DBUs (Databricks Units) for the compute you use, at a rate that depends on the compute type; classic compute also has the cloud provider's charge for the virtual machines. So: serverless or jobs compute for scheduled work, never all-purpose compute; set auto termination on all-purpose compute; and size SQL warehouses to the workload."}],"tables":[{"title":"Which compute for which work (our summary of the docs)","head":["Compute","Starts","Best for","Not for","You pay for"],"rows":[["Serverless (notebooks, jobs, pipelines)","Seconds","Most notebooks, jobs and pipelines","R, RDDs, custom Spark or machine settings","Use only"],["Classic jobs compute","Minutes","Scheduled jobs that need custom settings","Interactive work","The run, at the lower jobs rate, plus VMs"],["Classic all-purpose compute","Minutes","Interactive development, R, RDDs","Scheduled production jobs","Uptime until auto termination, plus VMs"],["Serverless SQL warehouse","About 2 to 6 seconds","BI, dashboards, SQL tasks, SQL ETL","Python or Scala code","Use only"],["Pro SQL warehouse","About 4 minutes","SQL where serverless is not available, or with custom networking","Bursty use","Uptime, plus VMs"]]}],"problems":[{"t":"Pick compute for five workloads","q":"Choose compute for: (a) a nightly PySpark job with default settings; (b) 40 analysts running dashboards all day; (c) a data scientist exploring in R; (d) a scheduled job that needs a specific GPU instance and an init script; (e) a quick SQL ad-hoc query.","steps":["(a) Scheduled, standard Python, no special settings: serverless compute for jobs. Nothing idles and nothing to size.","(b) Concurrent SQL from BI: a serverless SQL warehouse, which scales with the number of queries and starts in seconds.","(c) R is not available on serverless or Standard access mode: classic all-purpose compute in Dedicated access mode, with auto termination.","(d) Specific instance types and init scripts need classic: jobs compute, created for the run, at the lower jobs rate.","(e) An ad-hoc SQL query: the serverless SQL warehouse, or serverless notebook compute."],"answer":"(a) Serverless jobs. (b) Serverless SQL warehouse. (c) All-purpose, Dedicated access mode. (d) Classic jobs compute. (e) Serverless SQL warehouse.","code":null},{"t":"Why the bill is high","q":"A team runs its scheduled jobs on an all-purpose cluster that is left on, so the jobs start fast. The monthly bill is far above plan. What would you change?","steps":["All-purpose compute charges for every minute it is up, including idle hours between jobs, and at a higher rate than jobs compute.","Move the jobs to serverless compute for jobs: it starts in seconds, so the reason for keeping a cluster up disappears, and it charges only while running.","If a job needs classic, use jobs compute, which is created for the run and shut down after it.","Keep all-purpose compute for interactive work only, with auto termination set to a short idle period."],"answer":"Move scheduled jobs to serverless (or classic jobs compute) and keep all-purpose compute for interactive work with auto termination.","code":null},{"t":"What Delta adds to Parquet","q":"An engineer says \"Delta is just Parquet\". Two writers update the same table at once, a bad load must be undone, and a column of the wrong type must be rejected. What does Delta Lake provide that plain Parquet files do not?","steps":["The transaction log records every change as an ordered commit, so readers see a consistent snapshot and concurrent writers cannot leave half-written results: ACID transactions.","Every commit is a version. DESCRIBE HISTORY lists them; you can query an older version, or RESTORE the table to one, which undoes the bad load.","Schema enforcement rejects writes whose columns do not match the table; schema evolution must be asked for explicitly.","MERGE, UPDATE and DELETE work on top of write-once Parquet files because the log records which files are current.","VACUUM removes files no longer in the log after the retention period (7 days by default), which also limits how far back time travel can go."],"answer":"The transaction log gives ACID writes, versions for time travel and RESTORE, schema enforcement, and MERGE, UPDATE and DELETE.","code":"DESCRIBE HISTORY main.silver.orders;\nSELECT * FROM main.silver.orders VERSION AS OF 41;\nRESTORE TABLE main.silver.orders TO VERSION AS OF 41;"},{"t":"Where things run and live","q":"A security reviewer asks: where does our code run, and where is our table data stored, when we use (a) serverless jobs and (b) classic jobs compute?","steps":["The control plane (web app, scheduler, Unity Catalog services) is run by Databricks in both cases.","(a) Serverless compute runs in the serverless compute plane in Databricks' account, in the same region as the workspace, inside isolation boundaries.","(b) Classic compute runs as virtual machines in your own cloud account, inside the workspace's network.","In both cases Unity Catalog tables and volumes live in cloud object storage (your own buckets for external locations, or managed storage), not on the compute."],"answer":"(a) Code runs in Databricks' serverless compute plane; (b) in your own cloud account. Table data stays in cloud object storage either way, governed by Unity Catalog.","code":null},{"t":"Naming and finding data","q":"A new engineer cannot find the orders table and asks why the same name works in one workspace but not another, and where raw CSV files should go.","steps":["In Unity Catalog every table has a three-part name: catalog.schema.table, such as main.sales.orders. Use the full name rather than relying on the current catalog.","Workspaces attached to the same metastore see the same catalogs, subject to grants and to which catalogs are bound to which workspaces.","If the name is right but the table does not appear, check privileges: BROWSE shows that it exists; USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA and SELECT are needed to read it.","Put raw files in a Unity Catalog volume, for example /Volumes/main/landing/raw/, so file access is governed like table access."],"answer":"Use catalog.schema.table names, check the workspace's catalog access and the user's privileges, and land raw files in a volume.","code":"SELECT * FROM main.sales.orders LIMIT 10;\nLIST '/Volumes/main/landing/raw/';"}],"mistakes":["Running scheduled jobs on all-purpose compute, which charges while idle and at a higher rate.","Picking classic compute out of habit when serverless supports the workload.","Expecting R or RDD code to run on serverless or Standard access mode.","Forgetting auto termination on all-purpose compute.","Thinking table data lives on the cluster; it lives in cloud object storage.","Using two-part names and getting the wrong catalog."],"links":[{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/getting-started/high-level-architecture","t":"High-level architecture","why":"The control plane, the serverless and classic compute planes, and where data lives."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/getting-started/concepts","t":"Databricks components","why":"The main parts of the platform in one place."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/choose-compute","t":"Compute selection recommendations","why":"Which compute Databricks recommends for notebooks, jobs, pipelines and SQL."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/sql-warehouse/warehouse-types","t":"SQL warehouse types","why":"Serverless, Pro and Classic compared."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/configure","t":"Compute configuration reference","why":"Access modes, autoscaling, auto termination and instance types."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/compute","t":"Configure compute for jobs","why":"Serverless, jobs compute and why not all-purpose for jobs."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/history","t":"Work with table history","why":"Delta versions, time travel and RESTORE."}],"checked":"Checked against the exam guide of 6 August 2026 and the Databricks docs (AWS) on 2 November 2026. Prices and DBU rates change and differ by cloud and region; the docs pages above do not give them, so check the Databricks pricing page and your own account."}},{"id":"ingest","name":"Data ingestion and loading","w":21,"study":{"cert":"dbx-dea","dom":"ingest","intro":"This section is 21% of the exam. It asks you to load data into Unity Catalog tables with the right Databricks tool, and to know what happens when files repeat, schemas drift, or the source is a database or an API. Work the solved problems with the answer hidden, then practise.","expects":["Describe batch, streaming and incremental ingestion, and load a local file.","Load new files incrementally into a Unity Catalog table with COPY INTO.","Run Auto Loader as a scheduled batch job, with schema enforcement and schema evolution.","Set up Lakeflow Connect to ingest from enterprise sources.","Pull from a database (JDBC or ODBC) or a REST API in a notebook that Lakeflow Jobs runs.","Choose between Auto Loader, Lakeflow Connect, partner connectors and other methods from volume, frequency, data type and governance.","Load JSON and other semi-structured data into Unity Catalog Delta tables."],"ideas":[{"t":"Three layers, start at the top","b":"Databricks' own advice is to start with the most managed option that supports your source and move down only when it cannot do the job. Top: Lakeflow Connect managed connectors. Middle: Lakeflow pipelines (declarative). Bottom: Structured Streaming with Spark APIs, the most control and the most code."},{"t":"Incremental means remembering","b":"To load only what is new, a tool must remember what it already loaded. Auto Loader remembers in its checkpoint, so a new or deleted checkpoint makes it forget and reload. COPY INTO keeps track of the files it loaded into the table. Managed database connectors read the source's change log."},{"t":"Files: Auto Loader or COPY INTO","b":"The docs' rule of thumb: COPY INTO suits files in the thousands over time; Auto Loader suits millions or more, discovers files with fewer operations, and handles schema change better. COPY INTO is easier when you need to reload a subset of files. For SQL users loading from cloud storage, the docs now recommend CREATE STREAMING TABLE."},{"t":"Three ways Auto Loader finds new files","b":"File events (preferred): Auto Loader reads a cache of storage events and does not list the folder each time; needs a Unity Catalog external location with file events turned on, and cloudFiles.useManagedFileEvents set to true. Classic file notification: its own queue per stream, a bit lower latency. Directory listing: simplest, fine for small folders and one-off backfills, the highest listing cost at scale."},{"t":"Run style: availableNow","b":"trigger(availableNow=True) processes whatever is available, then stops, so you pay for compute only while it runs. Schedule it with a Lakeflow Job or start it with a file arrival trigger. Use a continuous trigger only when you need low latency; it costs more. Trigger.Once is deprecated, and serverless compute supports only availableNow."},{"t":"Databases and APIs","b":"Managed database connectors (MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQL Server, Oracle) capture changes with CDC: a connection, an always-on ingestion gateway, a staging volume and an ingestion pipeline that fills streaming tables. With no connector, write a notebook that uses JDBC or a REST client, and run it as a Lakeflow Jobs task. If you do not need to copy the data at all, Lakehouse Federation queries it in place."},{"t":"Semi-structured data","b":"A VARIANT column (JSON needs Databricks Runtime 15.3 or above) keeps a whole record flexible when its shape changes. Keep stable, frequently filtered fields as typed columns, because VARIANT cannot be a partition or clustering key. Images and PDFs load as binaryFile."}],"tables":[{"title":"Which tool? (our summary of the docs' guidance)","head":["Situation","Use","Why"],"rows":[["Millions of files in a folder or volume, growing fast","Auto Loader, with file events","Scales, cheap discovery, strong schema evolution"],["Thousands of files, SQL-only team, scheduled","CREATE STREAMING TABLE (or COPY INTO)","Simple and incremental; the docs prefer streaming tables for SQL users"],["Reload a few corrected files","COPY INTO with FILES and force","Idempotent by default; force switches that off"],["One small file from a laptop, one time","File upload (Add data)","Up to 10 files, under 2 GB, no archives; not for repeat loads"],["Salesforce, Workday and similar apps","Lakeflow Connect SaaS connector","Managed authentication, incremental reads, schema changes"],["MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQL Server or Oracle with changes","Lakeflow Connect database connector (CDC)","Reads change logs; no custom code"],["A database with no connector","JDBC in a notebook run by a Lakeflow Job","Full control; you handle increments and retries"],["A REST API","Notebook lands raw JSON in a volume; Auto Loader loads it","Keeps API quirks away from transformations"],["Kafka, Kinesis, Pub/Sub","Structured Streaming or pipeline streaming source","These are streaming sources, not file sources"]]},{"title":"Auto Loader schema evolution modes (cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode)","head":["Mode","New column arrives","Notes"],"rows":[["addNewColumns","Stream stops once, saves the column, uses it after restart","Default when you give no schema"],["addNewColumnsWithTypeWidening","Same, and widens types such as int to long","Public Preview on Runtime 16.4 and above"],["rescue","Schema never changes, stream never stops; new data goes to _rescued_data","Safest for never losing data"],["failOnNewColumns","Stream fails and stays down until you update the schema or remove the file","A strict contract"],["none","New columns are ignored; the stream keeps running","Default when you give a schema; data is rescued only if rescuedDataColumn is set"]]}],"problems":[{"t":"Pick the tool for five sources","q":"A team must load: (a) about 3 million JSON files a day into cloud storage; (b) 3,000 CSV files a week into a volume, from a team that only writes SQL; (c) Salesforce accounts; (d) a SQL Server table that changes all day; (e) a partner's REST API with no Databricks connector. Choose a tool for each.","steps":["Start at the top layer: is there a managed connector for the source? (c) and (d) are both managed-connector sources.","(a) is a file source at very high volume. Auto Loader, preferably with file events, so it does not list a huge folder on every run.","(b) is a file source at modest volume and the team writes SQL. CREATE STREAMING TABLE over the volume is the docs' recommendation; COPY INTO also works at thousands of files.","(d) changes all day, so use the database connector (CDC): it reads the change log, so updates arrive without a custom pull.","(e) has no connector, so you build it: a notebook calls the API and writes raw JSON to a volume, and Auto Loader loads those files."],"answer":"(a) Auto Loader with file events. (b) A streaming table, or COPY INTO. (c) Salesforce connector. (d) Database connector with CDC. (e) Notebook to a volume, then Auto Loader, run by a Lakeflow Job."},{"t":"COPY INTO runs twice, and a file was corrected","q":"COPY INTO loaded 500 files yesterday. Today 20 new files arrived, and one of yesterday's files was corrected in place. What loads, and how do you reload the corrected file?","steps":["COPY INTO is idempotent: it skips files it has already loaded, even if the file was modified afterwards. So today only the 20 new files load, and the corrected file is skipped.","To reload on purpose, set the force copy option to true. That switches idempotency off for every file the command matches, so name the file with FILES (or narrow with PATTERN). Without that, all 520 files load again and create duplicates.","Force adds new rows; it does not remove the rows the old copy of that file produced. Delete those first, or load into a staging table and MERGE."],"answer":"Only the 20 new files load. To reload the corrected one, run COPY INTO with FILES set to that file and COPY_OPTIONS force = true, after removing its old rows.","code":"COPY INTO main.sales.orders_bronze\nFROM '/Volumes/main/sales/landing/orders/'\nFILEFORMAT = JSON\nFILES = ('orders_2026-11-01.json')\nCOPY_OPTIONS ('force' = 'true');"},{"t":"Auto Loader as a scheduled batch job","q":"JSON files land in /Volumes/main/sales/landing/orders/. Load only new files into main.sales.orders_bronze every hour, with no always-on cluster.","steps":["Read with the cloudFiles source and set cloudFiles.format. Set cloudFiles.schemaLocation so Auto Loader can store the inferred schema.","Write with a checkpointLocation. The checkpoint is how Auto Loader remembers which files it has processed: one checkpoint per stream, kept outside the source folder.","Add trigger(availableNow=True) so the run processes everything that has arrived and then stops.","Run it as a Lakeflow Job on an hourly schedule, or with a file arrival trigger so compute starts only when files land.","If a backlog is large, cloudFiles.maxFilesPerTrigger or maxBytesPerTrigger caps each micro-batch so batches stay a steady size."],"answer":"A cloudFiles readStream with schemaLocation, written with a checkpoint and trigger(availableNow=True), scheduled as a Lakeflow Job. In a Lakeflow pipeline the pipeline manages the schema and checkpoint locations for you.","code":"(spark.readStream\n  .format(\"cloudFiles\")\n  .option(\"cloudFiles.format\", \"json\")\n  .option(\"cloudFiles.schemaLocation\", \"/Volumes/main/sales/meta/orders_schema\")\n  .load(\"/Volumes/main/sales/landing/orders/\")\n .writeStream\n  .option(\"checkpointLocation\", \"/Volumes/main/sales/meta/orders_chk\")\n  .trigger(availableNow=True)\n  .toTable(\"main.sales.orders_bronze\"))"},{"t":"A new column arrives","q":"The stream above runs with default settings and no schema supplied. Today's files contain a new field, coupon_code. What happens, and what do you do?","steps":["With no schema supplied, the default mode is addNewColumns. Auto Loader notices the unknown field, adds coupon_code to the schema stored at the schema location, and stops the stream with an unknown-field error.","Nothing is broken. On the next start the stream uses the new schema and the new column is populated from then on; older rows hold NULL.","So run schema-evolving streams under a Lakeflow Job with retries: the first failure fixes the schema and the retry succeeds.","If you never want it to stop, use rescue: the schema stays fixed and the new field lands in _rescued_data. If a new field should be an incident, use failOnNewColumns."],"answer":"The stream stops once after saving the new column and works after a restart, which a job retry does for you. Use rescue to avoid stopping, or failOnNewColumns to demand attention."},{"t":"Bad values and the rescued data column","q":"You tell Auto Loader that amount is a DOUBLE, but some records contain \"N/A\". Where does the bad value go, and how do you look at it?","steps":["A schema hint types the column. By default JSON columns are inferred as strings, so a mismatch only appears once you hint or provide a type.","Values that do not fit go to the rescued data column, _rescued_data, as a JSON blob that also holds the source file path. The stream does not fail on a type mismatch.","Broken JSON structure is a different problem: that is a corrupt record, and the docs advise capturing it with columnNameOfCorruptRecord rather than badRecordsPath.","Query the blob with the colon path syntax and filter on rows where it is not NULL, then fix the source or reprocess."],"answer":"Into _rescued_data, as JSON with the original value and the file path. Query it with colon paths and WHERE _rescued_data IS NOT NULL.","code":"# Python: type amount, keep everything else as inferred\n.option(\"cloudFiles.schemaHints\", \"amount DOUBLE\")\n\n-- SQL: look at what did not fit\nSELECT order_id, _rescued_data:amount AS raw_amount\nFROM main.sales.orders_bronze\nWHERE _rescued_data IS NOT NULL;"},{"t":"Reading a database with JDBC, then only the changes","q":"Copy an 80-million-row orders table from a relational database into Delta every night, in parallel. Then change it to load only new and changed rows.","steps":["A plain JDBC read uses one connection. For a parallel read set partitionColumn (a numeric, date or timestamp column), lowerBound, upperBound and numPartitions. The bounds only decide how the range is cut into numPartitions queries; they do not filter, so rows outside the range are still read.","Keep numPartitions modest: each partition is a connection to a production database.","Never put credentials in the URL. Use the Unity Catalog JDBC connection, or read them with dbutils.secrets.get.","For increments, remember a watermark: the highest updated_at you loaded last time. Read rows above it, then MERGE into the target on the key so a rerun does not duplicate rows.","A watermark pull cannot see deleted rows. That is a reason to prefer a managed CDC connector when the source is supported."],"answer":"A JDBC read with partitionColumn, bounds and numPartitions, credentials from secrets or a UC connection, then a watermark filter and MERGE for the increment.","code":"df = (spark.read.format(\"jdbc\")\n  .option(\"url\", dbutils.secrets.get(\"ingest\", \"orders_url\"))\n  .option(\"dbtable\", \"public.orders\")\n  .option(\"user\", dbutils.secrets.get(\"ingest\", \"orders_user\"))\n  .option(\"password\", dbutils.secrets.get(\"ingest\", \"orders_pw\"))\n  .option(\"partitionColumn\", \"order_id\")\n  .option(\"lowerBound\", \"1\").option(\"upperBound\", \"80000000\")\n  .option(\"numPartitions\", \"8\")\n  .load())"},{"t":"Landing a REST API","q":"A partner API returns 100 records a page with a next_page token and allows 60 calls a minute. Land it every day.","steps":["The docs' decoupled pattern: a notebook or script, run as a scheduled Lakeflow Jobs task, calls the API and writes each response unchanged as JSON to a volume. A pipeline or Auto Loader then loads those files.","Why split it: API quirks (paging, rate limits, retries) stay in one small task, and Auto Loader's file tracking stops files being processed twice.","Follow next_page until it is empty. On a 429 reply wait and retry; cap the retries so a bad day fails the task instead of looping.","Read the token with dbutils.secrets.get, never from the notebook text.","Name files by run date and page number. A rerun then overwrites the same files instead of adding duplicates."],"answer":"Job task: page through the API with retries, write raw JSON to a dated volume folder; Auto Loader loads that folder into a bronze table.","code":"import json, time, requests\ntoken = dbutils.secrets.get(\"partner\", \"api_token\")\nrun_date = \"2026-11-02\"  # passed in by the job\nurl, params, page = \"https://api.example.com/v1/invoices\", {\"page_size\": 100}, 0\nwhile True:\n    r = requests.get(url, headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token}\"}, params=params, timeout=30)\n    if r.status_code == 429:\n        time.sleep(int(r.headers.get(\"Retry-After\", 10))); continue  # add a retry cap\n    r.raise_for_status()\n    body = r.json()\n    dbutils.fs.put(f\"/Volumes/main/sales/landing/invoices/{run_date}/page-{page:05d}.json\", json.dumps(body), True)\n    if not body.get(\"next_page\"): break\n    params[\"page_token\"], page = body[\"next_page\"], page + 1"},{"t":"A managed database connector: parts, compute and cost","q":"A SQL Server orders table changes all day. The business wants it in Unity Catalog within the hour without code to maintain. Which parts are created, where do they run, and what is the cost catch?","steps":["A Unity Catalog connection stores the database credentials.","The ingestion gateway runs continuously on classic compute. It extracts the first snapshot, then the change logs and metadata, before the source can truncate its logs.","A staging volume, created with the gateway, holds the extracted data until it is applied. Staging data is purged after 30 days.","The ingestion pipeline runs on serverless compute, on the schedule you choose, and applies staged changes to streaming tables.","Cost catch: the gateway is billed while it runs, even when the pipeline is idle. Also size its compute for the first snapshot, which can fail if undersized."],"answer":"Connection, gateway (classic, always on), staging volume, ingestion pipeline (serverless) writing streaming tables. You pay for the gateway even when the pipeline is idle. Check the connector page for how deletes and history are handled."},{"t":"Semi-structured JSON: VARIANT, struct or string?","q":"Clickstream JSON has stable event_id, ts and user_id fields and a properties object whose keys change every week. Load it into a Unity Catalog table.","steps":["Option 1: the whole record in one VARIANT column (Auto Loader singleVariantColumn). Most flexible, but there is no schema evolution and no rescued data column, and a record over 16 MB counts as corrupt.","Option 2: type the stable fields and keep only the changing part as VARIANT. Declare the VARIANT column in cloudFiles.schemaHints.","Why option 2: typed columns filter faster, and a VARIANT column cannot be a partition, clustering or Z-order key. The docs say pulling frequently queried fields out of VARIANT speeds queries and improves storage layout.","A raw string with from_json only makes sense when you must keep the exact text; it parses on every read.","Query inside VARIANT with the colon path and cast with ::."],"answer":"Typed event_id, ts and user_id plus properties as VARIANT, set through schemaHints; read fields with properties:campaign::string.","code":".option(\"cloudFiles.schemaHints\", \"event_id STRING, ts TIMESTAMP, user_id STRING, properties VARIANT\")\n\nSELECT event_id, properties:campaign::string AS campaign\nFROM main.web.events_bronze\nWHERE ts >= current_date() - 7;"}],"mistakes":["Sharing one checkpoint across two sources, or moving a stream to a new checkpoint and being surprised that every file loads again.","Deleting the schema location and expecting the old schema history to survive.","Treating COPY INTO and Auto Loader as interchangeable at any scale. Thousands of files: either. Millions: Auto Loader.","Running force on COPY INTO without FILES or PATTERN, which reloads everything and duplicates rows.","Putting credentials in a JDBC URL, or in notebook text instead of secrets.","Applying a cloud lifecycle policy to a checkpoint folder. Deleting checkpoint files corrupts the stream.","Ingesting everything as VARIANT and then querying it as if it were typed columns."],"links":[{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/","t":"Choose a standard connector","why":"The docs' own layers: managed, pipelines, Structured Streaming. Read the table of sources."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/","t":"Ingest data from cloud object storage","why":"Where the docs compare Auto Loader and COPY INTO by scale."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/schema","t":"Auto Loader schema inference and evolution","why":"The five evolution modes, the rescued data column and schema hints."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/file-events-explained","t":"Auto Loader with file events","why":"How file events replace listing, and when a full listing still happens."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/delta-copy-into","t":"COPY INTO reference","why":"Exact options: FILES, PATTERN, force, mergeSchema."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/lakeflow-connect/cdc-overview","t":"Managed database connectors","why":"The gateway, staging volume and ingestion pipeline, and what each costs."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/connect/jdbc-connection","t":"JDBC connection","why":"Unity Catalog JDBC connections and pushdown. Read the part on credentials."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/api-ingestion","t":"Ingest data from an API in pipelines","why":"The three patterns, including the decoupled one used above."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/variant","t":"Ingest data as VARIANT","why":"Runtime requirements, limits and the Auto Loader option."}],"checked":"Checked against the exam guide of 6 August 2026 and the Databricks docs (AWS) on 2 November 2026. Where a detail was not confirmed on a docs page, check it in a free Databricks workspace."}},{"id":"transform","name":"Data transformation and modelling","w":22,"study":{"cert":"dbx-dea","dom":"transform","intro":"This is the largest section, 22% of the exam. It takes a bronze table to silver and gold: cleaning, joining, reshaping, de-duplicating and aggregating with PySpark or SQL, tuning a few Spark settings, picking the right gold object for BI, and keeping the data trustworthy with quality rules. Work the solved problems with the answer hidden, then practise.","expects":["Clean a bronze table with PySpark or SQL: handle nulls, fix data types, and save the result as a silver table.","Combine DataFrames with inner, left, broadcast, multi-key and cross joins, and with union and union all.","Reshape data: add, drop, split and rename columns, filter rows, and explode arrays.","Remove duplicates and aggregate with counts, approximate distinct counts, means and summaries.","Tune basic Spark settings (shuffle partitions, default parallelism, executor and driver memory, the auto-broadcast threshold), then measure again.","Explain the gold-layer objects in Unity Catalog (tables, views, materialized views, streaming tables) and build them for BI teams.","Apply data quality checks and validation rules to keep silver and gold reliable."],"ideas":[{"t":"Bronze, silver, gold","b":"Bronze keeps the raw data as it arrived, so you can rebuild. Silver is cleaned and conformed: right types, nulls handled, duplicates removed, keys you can join on. Gold is shaped for a use: aggregates, business models and the objects BI tools read. Layers are usually schemas or catalogs in Unity Catalog."},{"t":"Cleaning is mostly types and nulls","b":"Serverless compute runs with ANSI mode on, so cast('N/A' AS INT) raises an error instead of quietly returning NULL. try_cast returns NULL for a value that does not fit, which lets you keep the row and count the failures. Fill nulls with fillna (df.na.fill) when a default is correct, drop them with dropna when the row is useless, and never fill a null key."},{"t":"Joins: keys, row counts and shuffles","b":"Join on every column of the key (a multi-key join), and check row counts before and after: a left join should never lose rows from the left side, and a jump in rows means the right side has duplicate keys. Joining on a list of column names keeps one copy of each key column. A broadcast join copies a small table to every executor, so the large table is not shuffled. A cross join pairs every row with every row; use it only on purpose."},{"t":"union matches by position","b":"DataFrame union and SQL UNION ALL append rows and keep duplicates; SQL UNION also removes duplicates, which costs a shuffle. union matches columns by position, so the same columns in a different order land in the wrong place. unionByName matches by name, and allowMissingColumns=True fills a missing column with NULL."},{"t":"Duplicates: say which row wins","b":"dropDuplicates([\"order_id\"]) keeps one row per key but does not promise which one. When the latest version must win, number the rows with row_number() over a window partitioned by the key and ordered by the update time, then keep row 1. In SQL, QUALIFY does this in one statement."},{"t":"Spark settings: few, measured, and set in the right place","b":"spark.sql.shuffle.partitions (200 by default, auto on serverless) sets partitions after joins and aggregations. Adaptive query execution is on by default: it merges small shuffle partitions, splits skewed ones and can switch to a broadcast join at run time. Executor and driver memory belong to the compute configuration, not a running session. Serverless lets you set only a short list of properties. Change one thing, then measure again in the Spark UI or the query profile."},{"t":"Gold objects for BI","b":"A table stores data you change yourself (INSERT, MERGE). A view stores only its query and runs it every time it is read. A materialized view stores the result and is refreshed, incrementally when it can, which suits aggregates and joins that dashboards hit often. A streaming table processes each new row once, which suits append-only data. Metric views keep KPI definitions in one place."},{"t":"Quality rules","b":"In pipelines, expectations check each row: warn (the default) keeps bad rows and counts them, DROP ROW removes them, FAIL UPDATE stops the update. On ordinary Delta tables, NOT NULL and CHECK constraints reject bad writes. To keep bad rows for inspection, split the data with WHERE filters into a silver table and a quarantine table."}],"tables":[{"title":"Gold objects in Unity Catalog (our summary of the docs)","head":["Object","Stores data?","How it stays current","Good for"],"rows":[["Table","Yes","You write to it: INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE","Data you manage yourself, such as SCD dimensions"],["View","No, only the query","Runs its query every time it is read","A stable interface over tables without copying data"],["Materialized view","Yes, the query result","Refreshed on a schedule, on source change or in a pipeline; incremental when possible (needs serverless)","Aggregates and joins that dashboards read often"],["Streaming table","Yes","Each new source row processed once by its pipeline","Append-only data: ingestion, events, low latency"],["Temporary view","No","Lives for the notebook session, or a single query in Databricks SQL","Scratch steps inside one notebook"],["Metric view","No","Metrics defined once in YAML, queried with SQL","The same KPI definition across every dashboard"]]},{"title":"Expectation actions (pipelines and standalone streaming tables or materialized views)","head":["Action","SQL","Python","Bad rows","Metrics"],"rows":[["Warn (default)","CONSTRAINT c EXPECT (cond)","@dp.expect","Kept in the target","Counted"],["Drop","... ON VIOLATION DROP ROW","@dp.expect_or_drop","Removed before writing","Counted"],["Fail","... ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE","@dp.expect_or_fail","The update stops and its table write rolls back","None, because the update failed"]]},{"title":"Spark settings named in the exam guide","head":["Setting","What it controls","Where to set it"],"rows":[["spark.sql.shuffle.partitions","Partitions after a join or aggregation (200; auto on serverless)","Session (spark.conf.set or SET), compute, pipeline"],["spark.default.parallelism","Default partitions for RDD operations such as parallelize","Compute configuration, before the cluster starts"],["spark.executor.memory, spark.driver.memory","Memory for each executor and for the driver","Compute configuration; not settable on serverless"],["spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold","Largest table Spark broadcasts on its own when planning (10 MB in Apache Spark; -1 turns it off)","Session or compute. AQE also has its own run-time threshold (30 MB on Databricks)"]]}],"problems":[{"t":"Bronze to silver: types and nulls","q":"main.bronze.orders has every column as a string. quantity contains values like \"3\", \"\" and \"N/A\"; order_ts is text; some rows have no order_id. Build main.silver.orders on serverless compute.","steps":["Decide per column what a bad value means. A row with no order_id cannot be joined or de-duplicated, so drop it. A blank or unreadable quantity becomes NULL, and then 0 only if the business agrees that missing means none.","Serverless runs with ANSI mode on, so cast('N/A' AS INT) fails the whole query. Use try_cast, which returns NULL for values that do not fit.","Trim text before casting, and parse timestamps with an explicit format so a different locale does not guess wrong.","Count the rows that failed to cast, and keep the count in a check or a log. Silent NULLs are how bad data reaches gold.","Write the result as a managed Delta table with saveAsTable (or CREATE TABLE AS SELECT) under the silver schema."],"answer":"Filter out null order_id, try_cast quantity and amount, parse order_ts with to_timestamp and a format, fill quantity with 0 only if agreed, and save as main.silver.orders.","code":"SELECT order_id,\n       coalesce(try_cast(trim(quantity) AS INT), 0) AS quantity,\n       try_cast(amount AS DECIMAL(12,2)) AS amount,\n       to_timestamp(order_ts, 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss') AS order_ts\nFROM main.bronze.orders\nWHERE order_id IS NOT NULL"},{"t":"A multi-key left join that grew","q":"Orders (1,000,000 rows) are left-joined to prices on product_id and region. The result has 1,240,000 rows, and the result has two product_id columns. Explain both and fix them.","steps":["A left join keeps every left row, so it can never have fewer than 1,000,000 rows. More rows means some (product_id, region) pairs appear more than once in prices.","Check it: group prices by the join key and look for counts above 1. Often the table holds price history, so you must also pick the price that was valid at the order date, or keep only the current row.","Join on every column of the key. Joining on product_id alone would multiply rows across regions.","Two product_id columns appear when you join with an expression such as o.product_id == p.product_id. Joining on a list of column names keeps a single copy of each key."],"answer":"prices has duplicate keys; de-duplicate it (or filter to the valid price) before joining, join on [\"product_id\", \"region\"], and confirm the row count equals the left side.","code":"dup = prices.groupBy(\"product_id\", \"region\").count().filter(\"count > 1\")\n\nsilver = orders.join(prices_current, [\"product_id\", \"region\"], \"left\")\nassert silver.count() == orders.count()"},{"t":"Broadcast and cross joins","q":"A 5 TB sales table joins a 25 MB store table. The plan shows a sort merge join with a large shuffle. Separately, you need one row for every store for every day of 2026, including days with no sales.","steps":["Spark broadcasts a table on its own only below spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold (10 MB in Apache Spark; check the value in your workspace). At 25 MB the planner does not, although adaptive query execution may switch at run time if the measured size is under its own threshold.","Make it explicit: broadcast(stores) in PySpark or a /*+ BROADCAST(s) */ hint in SQL. Each executor gets a full copy, and the 5 TB table joins without a shuffle.","Do not raise the threshold carelessly: a broadcast table must fit in driver and executor memory, and a table that grows later can cause out-of-memory errors.","For the daily grid, cross join the 365 dates with the stores (365 times the number of stores), then left join the sales on store and date and fill missing totals with 0."],"answer":"Broadcast the store table with a hint. Build the grid with dates CROSS JOIN stores, then LEFT JOIN sales so days without sales show 0.","code":"from pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast, sequence, explode, to_date, lit\n\njoined = sales.join(broadcast(stores), \"store_id\")\n\ndays = spark.range(1).select(explode(sequence(to_date(lit(\"2026-01-01\")), to_date(lit(\"2026-12-31\")))).alias(\"day\"))\ngrid = days.crossJoin(stores.select(\"store_id\"))"},{"t":"Combining two feeds","q":"Two regions send orders. North has columns (order_id, amount, region); South has (region, order_id, amount, coupon). Combine them into one DataFrame.","steps":["north.union(south) matches by position. The columns are in a different order, and South has an extra column, so it either fails or puts values in the wrong columns.","unionByName matches by column name. allowMissingColumns=True fills coupon with NULL for North rows.","union (and SQL UNION ALL) keeps duplicates. If an order can arrive in both feeds, de-duplicate afterwards on the key rather than using SQL UNION, which compares whole rows and costs a shuffle."],"answer":"north.unionByName(south, allowMissingColumns=True), then de-duplicate on order_id if the feeds overlap.","code":"allorders = north.unionByName(south, allowMissingColumns=True)"},{"t":"Reshaping: split, rename, drop, explode","q":"Each bronze order row has customer_name like \"Asha Rao\", a column named ord_dt, an unused column _raw, and an items array that is empty for cancelled orders. Produce one row per item with first_name and last_name, and keep cancelled orders visible.","steps":["split(customer_name, ' ') returns an array; take element 0 and element 1 (or use element_at, which is 1-based). Names with more than two parts need a rule, so agree on one.","withColumnRenamed(\"ord_dt\", \"order_date\") renames; drop(\"_raw\", \"customer_name\") removes columns you no longer need.","explode turns each array element into a row but drops rows whose array is empty or NULL, so cancelled orders vanish. explode_outer keeps them with a NULL item.","Filter rows with filter or where. Put filters early so less data moves through the joins that follow."],"answer":"Split the name, rename ord_dt, drop the spare columns, and use explode_outer on items so cancelled orders stay as one row with a NULL item.","code":"from pyspark.sql.functions import split, col, explode_outer\n\nout = (orders\n  .withColumn(\"parts\", split(col(\"customer_name\"), \" \"))\n  .withColumn(\"first_name\", col(\"parts\")[0])\n  .withColumn(\"last_name\", col(\"parts\")[1])\n  .withColumnRenamed(\"ord_dt\", \"order_date\")\n  .withColumn(\"item\", explode_outer(\"items\"))\n  .drop(\"parts\", \"_raw\", \"customer_name\", \"items\"))"},{"t":"Keep the latest version of each order","q":"Bronze has several versions of each order_id, each with an updated_at. Silver must hold only the latest version.","steps":["dropDuplicates([\"order_id\"]) keeps one row per order, but which one is not guaranteed. It is right for exact retries, wrong for versions.","Number the versions: row_number() over a window partitioned by order_id and ordered by updated_at descending. Keep row 1.","row_number gives exactly one winner even when two versions share a timestamp. Add a second sort column, such as the file name or an ingest sequence, so the choice is repeatable.","In SQL, QUALIFY filters on the window result without a subquery.","If silver is loaded incrementally, MERGE the latest row per key into it instead of rewriting the table."],"answer":"Filter on row_number() = 1 over PARTITION BY order_id ORDER BY updated_at DESC, using QUALIFY in SQL.","code":"SELECT *\nFROM main.bronze.orders\nQUALIFY row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY order_id ORDER BY updated_at DESC, _ingest_seq DESC) = 1"},{"t":"Aggregations for a daily report","q":"For each day, report orders, orders with a coupon, average order value, and the number of distinct customers. Events run to billions of rows and a 2% error on distinct customers is acceptable. Also get a quick profile of the amount column.","steps":["count(*) counts rows; count(coupon) counts only non-null coupons. Using one for the other is a common mistake.","avg ignores NULLs. If missing amounts should count as 0, fill them first; otherwise say in the report that NULLs are excluded.","approx_count_distinct estimates with HyperLogLog++. Its rsd argument sets the allowed error (0.05 by default); smaller is more accurate and uses more memory. An exact count(DISTINCT) is much more expensive on billions of rows.","df.describe(\"amount\") gives count, mean, stddev, min and max; df.summary() adds percentiles. Both are quick checks, not report columns."],"answer":"groupBy the day with count(*), count(coupon), avg(amount) and approx_count_distinct(customer_id, 0.02); profile amount with summary().","code":"from pyspark.sql import functions as F\n\ndaily = (events.groupBy(F.to_date(\"ts\").alias(\"day\"))\n  .agg(F.count(\"*\").alias(\"orders\"),\n       F.count(\"coupon\").alias(\"with_coupon\"),\n       F.avg(\"amount\").alias(\"avg_value\"),\n       F.approx_count_distinct(\"customer_id\", 0.02).alias(\"customers\")))\n\nevents.select(\"amount\").summary().show()"},{"t":"Tuning a slow join, then measuring again","q":"A nightly job on a classic cluster joins two large tables and writes hundreds of tiny files; the Spark UI shows a few tasks running far longer than the rest. Which settings do you look at, and where do you change them?","steps":["Measure first: note the run time and look at the stage with the long tasks in the Spark UI (task durations, shuffle read, spill).","Check that adaptive query execution is on (it is by default). It merges small shuffle partitions, which reduces tiny files, and splits skewed partitions in sort merge joins.","spark.sql.shuffle.partitions is the starting count for shuffles. Setting it to auto lets Databricks choose from the data size. Change it in the session with spark.conf.set or SET.","If one side is small, broadcast it and the shuffle disappears. Watch the threshold and the memory it needs.","Spill or out-of-memory errors point to memory. spark.executor.memory and spark.driver.memory are compute settings, so change the cluster configuration (or pick a larger node) and restart. On serverless you cannot set them.","Change one setting at a time and run again; keep the change only if the numbers improve."],"answer":"Confirm AQE is on, tune spark.sql.shuffle.partitions (or auto) in the session, broadcast a small side, fix memory in the compute configuration, and re-measure after each change.","code":"spark.conf.set(\"spark.sql.shuffle.partitions\", \"auto\")\nspark.conf.get(\"spark.sql.shuffle.partitions\")\n\n-- SQL\nSET spark.sql.shuffle.partitions = 400;"},{"t":"Choosing gold objects for a BI team","q":"The BI team needs (a) daily revenue by region, read by 50 dashboards; (b) a cleaned customer list where analysts should see the latest data with no copy; (c) a live feed of payment events; (d) a hand-maintained table of regional targets. Which object for each?","steps":["(a) is an aggregate read often: a materialized view. It stores the result and refreshes incrementally when the query and sources allow, which needs serverless; on classic compute every refresh recomputes fully.","Schedule it in the definition (SCHEDULE EVERY 1 HOURS, or TRIGGER ON UPDATE to refresh when sources change), or refresh it from a pipeline.","(b) wants no copy and always-current results: a view. It runs its query on every read, so keep it simple or point it at a materialized view.","(c) is append-only and arrives continuously: a streaming table, which processes each new event once.","(d) is edited by people: a regular managed table that you update with INSERT or MERGE.","Grant the BI group SELECT on the gold objects plus USE CATALOG and USE SCHEMA, rather than on the silver tables underneath."],"answer":"(a) Materialized view on a schedule. (b) View. (c) Streaming table. (d) Managed table. Grant SELECT on these to the BI group.","code":"CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW main.gold.daily_revenue\nSCHEDULE EVERY 1 HOURS\nAS SELECT order_date, region, sum(amount) AS revenue\nFROM main.silver.orders\nGROUP BY order_date, region;"},{"t":"Quality rules: warn, drop, fail or quarantine","q":"A pipeline builds silver_payments. Rules: amount must be positive (bad rows should be removed but counted), currency must be present (keep the rows but track how many lack it), and payment_id must never be null (stop everything if it is). Analysts also want to see the removed rows.","steps":["Positive amount: ON VIOLATION DROP ROW. Rows are removed and the count shows in the pipeline's data quality metrics.","Currency present: an expectation with no ON VIOLATION clause. That is warn, the default: rows stay and are counted.","payment_id not null: ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE. The update stops and its write rolls back; no metrics are recorded because the update failed, so read the error in the event log.","Dropped rows are gone from silver. To let analysts see them, add a separate quarantine table that selects the rows failing the rule. Write each table in its own flow rather than splitting inside one foreachBatch.","Outside pipelines, Delta CHECK and NOT NULL constraints reject a write that breaks them."],"answer":"DROP ROW for amount, a plain EXPECT for currency, FAIL UPDATE for payment_id, plus a quarantine table built from the inverse of the amount rule.","code":"CREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE silver_payments (\n  CONSTRAINT positive_amount EXPECT (amount > 0) ON VIOLATION DROP ROW,\n  CONSTRAINT has_currency EXPECT (currency IS NOT NULL),\n  CONSTRAINT has_id EXPECT (payment_id IS NOT NULL) ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE\n) AS SELECT * FROM STREAM(bronze_payments);\n\nCREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE quarantine_payments\nAS SELECT * FROM STREAM(bronze_payments) WHERE NOT (amount > 0);"}],"mistakes":["Using cast on dirty text on serverless, where ANSI mode makes one bad value fail the whole query. Use try_cast and count the NULLs it creates.","Filling a null key with a default value, which joins unrelated rows together.","Not checking row counts after a left join, so duplicate keys on the right silently multiply rows.","Using union on DataFrames whose columns are in a different order. Use unionByName.","Using explode where empty arrays matter; those rows disappear. Use explode_outer.","Using dropDuplicates when the latest version must win. Use row_number over a window, or QUALIFY.","Mixing up count(*) and count(column), or forgetting that avg ignores NULLs.","Changing several Spark settings at once, or trying to set executor memory from a notebook session.","Pointing 50 dashboards at a heavy view that recomputes on every read, instead of a materialized view.","Expecting FAIL UPDATE to record quality metrics, or DROP ROW to keep the dropped rows somewhere."],"links":[{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/try_cast","t":"try_cast function","why":"What try_cast returns when a value does not fit, compared with cast."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/transform/join","t":"Work with joins on Databricks","why":"Join types for batch and streaming, and stream-static joins."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-hints","t":"Hints","why":"BROADCAST and other join hints in SQL."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/aqe","t":"Adaptive query execution","why":"What AQE changes at run time, its settings and their defaults."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/spark/conf","t":"Set Spark configuration properties","why":"Where to set a property, and the short list serverless allows."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-engineering/tables-views","t":"Tables and views in Databricks","why":"Tables, views, materialized views and streaming tables side by side."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/views","t":"What is a view?","why":"View types, temporary view scope, and the permissions a reader needs."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/incremental-refresh","t":"Incremental refresh for materialized views","why":"When a refresh is incremental, and how to see which method ran."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/concepts/streaming-tables","t":"Streaming tables","why":"Append-only sources, processing each row once, and the join caveat."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/expectations","t":"Manage data quality with pipeline expectations","why":"Warn, drop and fail, and where the metrics appear."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/transform/validate","t":"Clean and validate data","why":"CHECK and NOT NULL constraints and the quarantine pattern."}],"checked":"Checked against the exam guide of 6 August 2026 and the Databricks docs (AWS) on 2 November 2026. The Apache Spark default for spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold (10 MB) and the PySpark function details come from Spark itself; check them in a free Databricks workspace."}},{"id":"jobs","name":"Working with Lakeflow Jobs","w":16,"study":{"cert":"dbx-dea","dom":"jobs","intro":"Lakeflow Jobs is how Databricks runs work in order and on time, and it is 16% of the exam. You need to build a task graph, choose the right task type for each step, control the flow with dependencies, retries, branches and loops, and start the job with the right trigger. Work the solved problems with the answer hidden, then practise.","expects":["Build a task graph: notebook, SQL, dashboard and pipeline tasks connected by dependencies.","Control the flow with Run if conditions, retries, If/else branches and For each loops.","Pass values between tasks with task values and dynamic value references.","Schedule a job with a time-based schedule, a file arrival trigger or a table update trigger.","Decide when a time-based trigger fits and when a data-driven trigger fits, based on when the data really arrives.","Fix a failed run with a repair run instead of starting again."],"ideas":[{"t":"A job is a graph of tasks","b":"Each task does one step, and Depends on draws the arrows. Tasks with no dependency between them run in parallel; a task waits for everything it depends on. Keep tasks small enough that a failure points to one step and a repair reruns little."},{"t":"Pick the task type for the step","b":"A notebook task runs a notebook. A SQL task runs a query, a .sql file or an alert on a serverless or pro SQL warehouse. A dashboard task refreshes a published AI/BI dashboard and can email a snapshot to subscribers. A pipeline task starts one update of a Lakeflow pipeline, optionally a full refresh. A Run Job task starts another job, which lets you split a large flow into reusable jobs."},{"t":"Run if: when a task starts","b":"The default is All succeeded. Others are At least one succeeded, None failed, All done, At least one failed and All failed. A task whose condition is not met is marked Upstream failed (for the success conditions) or Excluded (for the failure conditions). All done is the usual choice for a cleanup or notification step that must always run."},{"t":"Retries, timeouts and repairs","b":"Task retries are off by default (max_retries is 0). Set the number of retries and the interval between them for steps that fail for passing reasons, such as a network error. A timeout marks a task Timed Out, and it applies to each retry. A repair run reruns only the failed tasks and the tasks that depend on them, with the current settings and any parameter changes you make. Reruns can write duplicate output, so make tasks safe to repeat with overwrite or MERGE."},{"t":"Passing values between tasks","b":"A Python notebook sets a value with dbutils.jobs.taskValues.set(key, value); it must be JSON-serialisable and at most 48 KiB. Later tasks read it with a dynamic value reference such as {{tasks.validate.values.bad_rows}} in a parameter. References like {{job.run_id}}, {{job.start_time.iso_date}} and {{job.parameters.region}} fill in run details."},{"t":"If/else and For each","b":"An If/else condition task compares two operands and sends the flow down its true or false branch. == and != compare as text, so 12.0 == 12 is false; >, >=, < and <= compare as numbers. A For each task runs one nested task once per input in a JSON array, reading the current item as {{input}}. Concurrency (1 by default, up to 100) sets how many run at once. A For each cannot contain another For each."},{"t":"Triggers: time or data","b":"A schedule runs at fixed times. A file arrival trigger checks a Unity Catalog volume or external location about once a minute and starts a run when new files appear. A table update trigger starts a run when one or all of up to 25 Unity Catalog tables change. Continuous keeps one run going all the time. Use a schedule when the data arrives at a known time or the business wants a fixed time; use a data-driven trigger when arrival times vary."},{"t":"Concurrency and queueing","b":"A job runs one run at a time by default (max_concurrent_runs is 1). With queueing on, which is the default for jobs created in the UI since April 2024, a run that cannot start waits in a queue for up to 48 hours instead of being skipped. A pipeline can run only one update at a time, so a job with a pipeline task is capped at one run."}],"tables":[{"title":"Run if conditions (default: All succeeded)","head":["Condition","API value","The task runs when","If not met"],"rows":[["All succeeded","ALL_SUCCESS","Every dependency ran and succeeded","Upstream failed"],["At least one succeeded","AT_LEAST_ONE_SUCCESS","At least one dependency succeeded","Upstream failed"],["None failed","NONE_FAILED","No dependency failed, and at least one ran","Upstream failed"],["All done","ALL_DONE","All dependencies finished, whatever their result","Always runs"],["At least one failed","AT_LEAST_ONE_FAILED","At least one dependency failed","Excluded"],["All failed","ALL_FAILED","Every dependency failed","Excluded"]]},{"title":"Triggers compared (our summary of the docs)","head":["Trigger","Starts a run when","Settings to know","Watch out for"],"rows":[["Scheduled","A set time arrives (cron schedule)","Schedule and time zone; Pause","Runs even if the data has not arrived"],["File arrival","New files appear in a UC volume or external location (checked about every minute)","Minimum time between triggers; Wait after last change (both 60 seconds or more)","Overwriting a file with the same name does not trigger; without file events, 50 jobs per workspace and 10,000 files per location"],["Table update","Any or all of up to 25 Unity Catalog tables change","Trigger when: Any or All; the same two timers","The Run as identity needs SELECT, USE CATALOG and USE SCHEMA, or the trigger silently fails to evaluate"],["Continuous","The previous run ends, so one run is always going","Retries use exponential backoff","Cannot be combined with other triggers"],["None (manual)","Someone clicks Run now, or a tool calls the API","Job parameters at run time","Nothing runs unless asked"]]}],"problems":[{"t":"Build the task graph","q":"A nightly flow must: ingest raw files, then build silver_orders and silver_customers (independent of each other), then build gold_sales from both, then refresh the sales dashboard. Design the job.","steps":["List the steps and their inputs. silver_orders and silver_customers both need only the ingest step, so they do not depend on each other and can run in parallel.","Draw the arrows: ingest, then the two silver tasks both depending on ingest, then gold_sales depending on both silver tasks, then the dashboard task depending on gold_sales.","Choose a task type per step. If the medallion steps are a Lakeflow pipeline, one pipeline task can replace the three table tasks, and the pipeline works out the order inside itself.","Use a dashboard task for the refresh. It needs a published dashboard and a serverless or pro SQL warehouse, and it can email subscribers a snapshot.","Leave every Run if at All succeeded, the default: gold must not build from half-finished silver."],"answer":"ingest, then silver_orders and silver_customers in parallel, then gold_sales (depends on both), then a dashboard task. All with Run if All succeeded.","code":"tasks:\n  - task_key: ingest\n    notebook_task: {notebook_path: ../src/ingest.py}\n  - task_key: silver_orders\n    depends_on: [{task_key: ingest}]\n    notebook_task: {notebook_path: ../src/silver_orders.py}\n  - task_key: silver_customers\n    depends_on: [{task_key: ingest}]\n    notebook_task: {notebook_path: ../src/silver_customers.py}\n  - task_key: gold_sales\n    depends_on: [{task_key: silver_orders}, {task_key: silver_customers}]\n    sql_task:\n      warehouse_id: ${var.warehouse_id}\n      file: {path: ../src/gold_sales.sql}\n  - task_key: refresh_dashboard\n    depends_on: [{task_key: gold_sales}]\n    dashboard_task: {dashboard_id: ${var.sales_dashboard_id}}"},{"t":"A cleanup step that always runs","q":"The job above writes temporary files to a volume. A cleanup task must delete them whether the run succeeds or fails, and an alert task must post to the support channel only if something failed. Set their Run if conditions.","steps":["With the default All succeeded, the cleanup task would be marked Upstream failed and skipped whenever anything fails, which is exactly when cleanup matters.","All done runs the task once all its dependencies have finished, whatever their result. Use it for cleanup.","At least one failed runs the task only if a dependency failed. If nothing failed, the task is Excluded, which is not an error.","Make both tasks depend on the last real step, and on any branch that might fail earlier, so they see every failure."],"answer":"Cleanup: Run if All done. Alert: Run if At least one failed.","code":"  - task_key: cleanup\n    depends_on: [{task_key: refresh_dashboard}]\n    run_if: ALL_DONE\n    notebook_task: {notebook_path: ../src/cleanup.py}\n  - task_key: alert_support\n    depends_on: [{task_key: refresh_dashboard}]\n    run_if: AT_LEAST_ONE_FAILED\n    notebook_task: {notebook_path: ../src/alert.py}"},{"t":"Retries for a flaky source","q":"An ingestion task calls a partner API. About once a week it fails with a short network error, and once it hung for six hours. What do you set on the task?","steps":["Retries are off by default (max_retries is 0). Set a small number, such as 3, for errors that pass on their own.","Add an interval between attempts (min_retry_interval_millis), for example 2 minutes, so a busy service has time to recover. Without it, the retry starts straight away.","Set a timeout, such as 30 minutes, so a hung call fails instead of holding the job for hours. The timeout applies to each retry.","A timed-out task is not retried unless retry_on_timeout is true, which is false by default. Turn it on only if a timeout is likely to be temporary too.","Make the task safe to repeat: write with MERGE or overwrite a partition, so a retry after a partial write does not create duplicates.","Add a duration Warning so a slow-but-alive run raises a notification before it reaches the timeout."],"answer":"max_retries 3 with a 2-minute interval, a 30-minute timeout (retry_on_timeout only if timeouts are transient), an idempotent write, and a duration warning.","code":"  - task_key: pull_partner_api\n    max_retries: 3\n    min_retry_interval_millis: 120000\n    timeout_seconds: 1800\n    retry_on_timeout: true\n    notebook_task: {notebook_path: ../src/pull_api.py}"},{"t":"Repair instead of rerun","q":"A 7-task run failed at task 5 after 2 hours, because the date parameter was wrong. Tasks 6 and 7 depend on task 5. Tasks 1 to 4 succeeded. How do you finish the run?","steps":["Run now would start all 7 tasks again and repeat 2 hours of work.","Repair run reruns the unsuccessful tasks and the tasks that depend on them: here 5, 6 and 7. The Repair job run dialog lists them before you confirm.","In the same dialog, change the date parameter. The new value overrides the old one for the rerun.","Reruns use the current job and task settings, so fix the notebook or settings first if the bug was there.","Repair needs a job with two or more tasks; a single-task job is simply run again.","Check that task 5 can safely run twice: it may have written some data before it failed."],"answer":"Use Repair run with the corrected date parameter. Only tasks 5, 6 and 7 rerun; tasks 1 to 4 keep their results.","code":null},{"t":"Branch on a validation result","q":"A validate task counts rows that break the rules. If the count is above zero, the job must run a quarantine task; otherwise it must publish to gold. Build this without editing the job each time.","steps":["In the validate notebook, store the count as a task value: dbutils.jobs.taskValues.set(\"bad_rows\", n). Values must be JSON-serialisable and at most 48 KiB.","Add an If/else condition task that depends on validate. Left operand: {{tasks.validate.values.bad_rows}}. Operator: >. Right operand: 0.","Use > rather than != for numbers. == and != compare as text, so \"0.0\" != \"0\" would be true even when nothing is wrong.","Make quarantine depend on the condition task with outcome true, and publish depend on it with outcome false. Only one branch runs.","If the task that sets bad_rows is disabled, the condition task fails, so keep validate enabled."],"answer":"validate sets a task value; an If/else task tests {{tasks.validate.values.bad_rows}} > 0; quarantine hangs off true, publish off false.","code":"# in the validate notebook\nn = spark.table(\"main.silver.orders_rejects\").count()\ndbutils.jobs.taskValues.set(key=\"bad_rows\", value=n)\n\n# job definition\n  - task_key: check\n    depends_on: [{task_key: validate}]\n    condition_task:\n      op: GREATER_THAN\n      left: \"{{tasks.validate.values.bad_rows}}\"\n      right: \"0\"\n  - task_key: quarantine\n    depends_on: [{task_key: check, outcome: \"true\"}]\n    notebook_task: {notebook_path: ../src/quarantine.py}\n  - task_key: publish\n    depends_on: [{task_key: check, outcome: \"false\"}]\n    notebook_task: {notebook_path: ../src/publish.py}"},{"t":"Loop over a list of countries","q":"The same load notebook must run for each country in a list that changes every month (today 30 countries). At most 5 should run at once because the source database is small.","steps":["Creating 30 notebook tasks by hand means editing the job whenever the list changes. A For each task runs one nested task once per input instead.","Produce the list in an earlier task and pass it as a task value, for example a JSON array of country codes. The Inputs box itself also accepts a literal JSON array (up to 5,000 characters).","Set the For each inputs to {{tasks.get_countries.values.countries}} and concurrency to 5 (the default is 1; the maximum is 100).","In the nested notebook task, pass {{input}} as a parameter, for example country = {{input}}. For a list of objects, {{input.code}} picks one field.","Retries go on the nested task, so one failed country can retry without the others. A For each cannot contain another For each.","Downstream tasks depend on the For each task, not the nested task, and can read each iteration's task values as a list."],"answer":"get_countries sets the list as a task value; a For each task with inputs {{tasks.get_countries.values.countries}} and concurrency 5 runs the load notebook with country = {{input}}.","code":"  - task_key: load_countries\n    depends_on: [{task_key: get_countries}]\n    for_each_task:\n      inputs: \"{{tasks.get_countries.values.countries}}\"\n      concurrency: 5\n      task:\n        task_key: load_one_country\n        max_retries: 2\n        notebook_task:\n          notebook_path: ../src/load_country.py\n          base_parameters: {country: \"{{input}}\"}"},{"t":"Run when files arrive","q":"A vendor uploads 20 to 40 CSV files to /Volumes/main/landing/vendor/ at random times, in bursts over about 10 minutes. The job should process each burst once, soon after it ends, and never run more often than every 15 minutes.","steps":["A schedule either runs too early (half the burst) or too late. A file arrival trigger on the volume path fits.","Set Wait after last change to about 300 seconds: the timer resets with each new file, so the run starts once the burst has gone quiet.","Set Minimum time between triggers to 900 seconds so runs are at least 15 minutes apart. Both settings must be 60 seconds or more.","The trigger only starts the job; it does not tell the job which files are new. Read them with Auto Loader and trigger(availableNow=True), which processes each file once and keeps its own checkpoint.","Overwriting a file with the same name does not count as a new file. Ask the vendor for unique file names.","Without file events on the location, a workspace can have only 50 jobs with file arrival triggers and the location can hold up to 10,000 files. Turn on file events on the external location for larger setups.","The job needs READ on the location, and you need CAN MANAGE on the job."],"answer":"A file arrival trigger on the volume path, Wait after last change 300 s, Minimum time between triggers 900 s, and Auto Loader with availableNow inside the job.","code":"trigger:\n  file_arrival:\n    url: /Volumes/main/landing/vendor/\n    wait_after_last_change_seconds: 300\n    min_time_between_triggers_seconds: 900\n\n# inside the job\n(spark.readStream.format(\"cloudFiles\")\n   .option(\"cloudFiles.format\", \"csv\")\n   .option(\"cloudFiles.schemaLocation\", ckpt)\n   .load(\"/Volumes/main/landing/vendor/\")\n   .writeStream.option(\"checkpointLocation\", ckpt)\n   .trigger(availableNow=True)\n   .toTable(\"main.bronze.vendor\"))"},{"t":"Run when upstream tables are ready","q":"A gold job reads main.silver.orders (owned by one team) and main.silver.payments (owned by another). Each finishes somewhere between 1 am and 4 am. Today the gold job runs at 5 am to be safe. Make it run as soon as both are ready.","steps":["A 5 am schedule wastes hours on good days and still fails on a late day. A table update trigger starts the job when the data changes.","Add both tables and set Trigger when to All tables are updated, so the job waits for both. Any table is updated would start it after the first one.","Each team may write several commits in a row. Set Wait after last change, such as 600 seconds, so the run starts after the writes settle.","The job's Run as identity needs SELECT on both tables plus USE CATALOG and USE SCHEMA. Without them the trigger fails to evaluate and the job never runs, with no error shown unless you set failure notifications.","Inside the job, {{job.trigger.table_update.updated_tables}} lists the tables that changed, and each table's version and commit time are available too, which helps with incremental reads.","A trigger watches up to 25 tables. More than 10 needs file events on every table."],"answer":"A table update trigger on both tables with Trigger when All tables are updated, a wait-after-last-change timer, SELECT and USE privileges for the Run as identity, and failure notifications.","code":"trigger:\n  table_update:\n    table_names: [main.silver.orders, main.silver.payments]\n    condition: ALL_UPDATED\n    wait_after_last_change_seconds: 600"},{"t":"Time-based or data-driven?","q":"Choose a trigger for each: (a) a finance report that must be in inboxes at 8 am every working day; (b) partner files that arrive any time between midnight and noon; (c) a gold table that should refresh whenever its silver source changes; (d) a fraud model that must score events within seconds.","steps":["(a) The business wants a fixed time, and the source data is complete well before. A schedule, with a duration warning so a slow run is caught before 8 am.","(b) Arrival time varies by hours. A file arrival trigger runs as soon as the files land.","(c) The dependency is on another table, not a time. A table update trigger, or, if both live in the same Lakeflow pipeline, let the pipeline handle the order.","(d) Seconds of delay rules out starting a new run for each change. Use a continuous job (or a continuous pipeline) with Structured Streaming.","Rule of thumb: if you are adding \"safety margin\" hours to a schedule, a data-driven trigger is the better fit."],"answer":"(a) Scheduled. (b) File arrival. (c) Table update. (d) Continuous.","code":null}],"mistakes":["Leaving a cleanup or alert task on the default All succeeded, so it is skipped exactly when something fails.","Expecting retries by default. max_retries is 0 unless you set it.","Expecting a timed-out task to retry. retry_on_timeout is false by default.","Using Run now after a failure in a long job instead of Repair run, which reruns only the failed and dependent tasks.","Comparing numbers with == or != in an If/else task; they compare as text.","Hard-coding 30 tasks instead of one For each task, or nesting a For each inside another.","Relying on the trigger to say which files are new instead of using Auto Loader inside the job.","Forgetting that overwriting a file with the same name does not fire a file arrival trigger.","Choosing Any table is updated when the job needs all its sources, so it runs on half-ready data.","Forgetting SELECT and USE privileges for the job's Run as identity, so a table update trigger never fires."],"links":[{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/configure-task","t":"Configure and edit tasks in Lakeflow Jobs","why":"Task types, retries, timeouts and duration warnings."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/run-if","t":"Configure task dependencies","why":"Every Run if condition and how Excluded and Upstream failed are counted."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/tasks/if-else","t":"Add branching logic with the If/else task","why":"Operators, operand types and the true and false branches."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/tasks/for-each","t":"Use a For each task to run another task in a loop","why":"Inputs, concurrency, {{input}} and the limits on nesting."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/task-values","t":"Use task values to pass information between tasks","why":"set and get, the 48 KiB limit and debugValue."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/dynamic-value-references","t":"Dynamic value references","why":"The {{ }} references for job, run, parameters and task values."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/repair-job-failures","t":"Troubleshoot and repair job failures","why":"What a repair run reruns and how to change parameters."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/triggers","t":"Automate jobs with schedules and triggers","why":"Every trigger type, pausing, and concurrency."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/file-arrival-triggers","t":"Trigger jobs when new files arrive","why":"Locations, the two timers and the limits with and without file events."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/trigger-table-update","t":"Trigger jobs when source tables are updated","why":"Any or All, the privileges needed and the updated_tables parameters."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/tasks/dashboard","t":"Dashboard task for jobs","why":"Refreshing a published dashboard and emailing subscribers."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/tasks/pipeline","t":"Pipeline task for jobs","why":"Running a pipeline update, full refresh, and the one-update limit."}],"checked":"Checked against the exam guide of 6 August 2026, the Databricks docs (AWS) and the Jobs API reference on 2 November 2026. The YAML in the solved problems follows the bundle format; check field names against your CLI version."}},{"id":"cicd","name":"Implementing CI/CD","w":10,"study":{"cert":"dbx-dea","dom":"cicd","intro":"CI/CD is 10% of the exam: keeping notebooks and code in Git, and moving the same jobs and pipelines from dev to test to prod with bundles and the Databricks CLI. Bundles are now called Declarative Automation Bundles in the docs (formerly Databricks Asset Bundles); you may see either name, and the commands are the same. Work the solved problems with the answer hidden, then practise.","expects":["Work in a Git folder from the workspace: create and switch branches, commit and push, pull, and get changes reviewed through a pull request.","Describe jobs, pipelines and other workspace assets as code in a bundle.","Keep one codebase for dev, test and prod with targets, variables and overrides.","Validate, deploy and run a bundle with the Databricks CLI, by hand and from a CI pipeline."],"ideas":[{"t":"Git folders: Git inside the workspace","b":"A Git folder is a clone of a remote repository in the workspace. From its Git dialog you create a branch, switch branches, Commit & Push (one action, with a required message), Pull, Merge and Rebase. Pull requests are not created in the Git folder; you push the branch and open the pull request in your Git provider. Reset is git reset --hard plus a force push, so it throws away changes on the remote branch too."},{"t":"A bundle is the project as code","b":"databricks.yml at the root names the bundle and its targets, and resource files describe jobs, pipelines, dashboards and other assets. Source files (notebooks, Python, SQL) live next to them. Deploying puts all of it into a workspace as one unit, so what runs in prod is exactly what was reviewed in Git."},{"t":"Targets are environments","b":"targets: lists dev, test, prod and so on, each with its workspace host, mode and settings. One target has default: true and is used when you do not pass -t. mode: development prefixes resource names with [dev your_name], pauses schedules and triggers, and is meant for each developer's own copy. mode: production checks that run_as and permissions are set, does not allow overriding clusters, and can require a Git branch."},{"t":"Variables and overrides","b":"Declare variables at the top (with a default, or a lookup that turns a warehouse or policy name into its ID), use them as ${var.name}, and give each target its own values. Values can also come from --var or BUNDLE_VAR_ environment variables, which win over the file. A target can also override resource settings: they merge with the top-level ones by key (task_key, job_cluster_key), and the target value wins."},{"t":"The CLI lifecycle","b":"databricks bundle init starts from a template. validate checks the configuration. plan shows what a deploy would change. deploy uploads files and creates or updates resources. run starts a job or pipeline from the bundle. summary lists what is deployed. destroy removes it. Every command takes -t to pick the target."},{"t":"CI/CD flow","b":"Developers deploy to their own dev target. A pull request runs tests and bundle validate. A merge to main runs bundle deploy -t prod from the CI system, authenticated as a service principal, with run_as set to that service principal so production jobs never run as a person."}],"tables":[{"title":"Bundle CLI commands you need","head":["Command","What it does","Typical use"],"rows":[["databricks bundle init","Creates a project from a template","Starting a new bundle"],["databricks bundle validate -t dev","Checks the configuration and resolves variables","Before every deploy; first step in CI"],["databricks bundle plan -t prod","Shows what a deploy would change, without changing anything","Reviewing a production change"],["databricks bundle deploy -t prod","Uploads files and creates or updates jobs, pipelines and other resources","After a merge to main"],["databricks bundle run -t dev my_job","Runs a job or pipeline defined in the bundle","Testing after a deploy"],["databricks bundle summary -t prod","Lists deployed resources with workspace links","Checking what is live"],["databricks bundle destroy -t dev","Deletes the deployed resources","Removing a developer's copy"]]},{"title":"Development and production modes (our summary of the docs)","head":["","mode: development","mode: production"],"rows":[["Resource names","Prefixed [dev your_name] and tagged dev","As written"],["Schedules and triggers","Paused","As written"],["Concurrent runs","Allowed on all jobs","As written"],["Pipelines","Marked development","Must not be development"],["Cluster override (--cluster-id)","Allowed","Not allowed"],["Checks","Deployment lock off","run_as and permissions required unless a service principal deploys; Git branch must match git.branch if set"]]}],"problems":[{"t":"A feature branch in a Git folder","q":"You must change a notebook in the team repository, have it reviewed, and get it into main. The repository is already a Git folder in your workspace. List the steps.","steps":["Open the Git dialog for the folder and Pull, so you start from the latest main.","Create Branch, for example feature/fix-dedup, based on main. The folder switches to it.","Edit and test the notebook.","Enter a commit message and click Commit & Push. Committing and pushing are one action in the Git folder.","Open a pull request in the Git provider (GitHub, GitLab, Azure DevOps and so on). The Git folder does not create pull requests.","After review and merge, switch the folder back to main and Pull."],"answer":"Pull, Create Branch, edit, Commit & Push, open the pull request in the Git provider, then switch back to main and Pull after the merge.","code":null},{"t":"Conflicts and a bad reset","q":"Two colleagues edited the same notebook. When you merge main into your branch the Git folder reports a conflict. Separately, someone suggests Reset to fix a messy branch. What do you do?","steps":["The Git folder lists the conflicting files. For each one, edit it by hand, keep your changes, or take the incoming changes, then mark it resolved and continue the merge. Abort if you need to start again.","Reset in a Git folder is git reset --hard followed by a force push. It discards local changes and rewrites the remote branch too.","Use Reset only on a branch nobody else uses, and only when you really want the remote branch to match another state.","Several people working in one shared Git folder causes exactly these conflicts. Give each person their own Git folder."],"answer":"Resolve the listed conflicts in the UI and continue the merge. Avoid Reset on shared branches, because it force-pushes. Use one Git folder per person.","code":null},{"t":"One bundle, three environments","q":"The same job must deploy to a dev workspace and a prod workspace. It reads catalog dev_main in dev and main in prod, and uses a different SQL warehouse in each. Write the bundle skeleton.","steps":["Declare a variable catalog with no hard-coded environment in the job, and reference it as ${var.catalog}.","For the warehouse, a lookup variable resolves the warehouse ID from its name, so the YAML does not hold IDs that differ per workspace.","Under targets, give dev and prod their workspace host, mode and variable values. Make dev the default target.","Pass ${var.catalog} to the job as a job parameter, so the notebook reads it at run time. Bundle variables are resolved at deploy time; job runs need parameters."],"answer":"Variables catalog and warehouse_id (by lookup), referenced as ${var.*} in the job, with values set per target; dev default in development mode, prod in production mode.","code":"bundle:\n  name: sales_etl\n\nvariables:\n  catalog:\n    description: Catalog for this environment\n  warehouse_id:\n    lookup:\n      warehouse: \"etl-warehouse\"\n\nresources:\n  jobs:\n    daily_sales:\n      name: daily_sales\n      parameters:\n        - name: catalog\n          default: ${var.catalog}\n      tasks:\n        - task_key: gold\n          sql_task:\n            warehouse_id: ${var.warehouse_id}\n            file: {path: ./src/gold.sql}\n\ntargets:\n  dev:\n    default: true\n    mode: development\n    workspace: {host: https://dev.cloud.databricks.com}\n    variables: {catalog: dev_main}\n  prod:\n    mode: production\n    workspace: {host: https://prod.cloud.databricks.com}\n    variables: {catalog: main}"},{"t":"Bigger compute only in prod","q":"The job cluster has 2 workers. In prod it needs 8 workers; everything else stays the same. Do it without copying the whole job into the prod target.","steps":["Target resources merge with top-level resources. Repeat only the job, the job_cluster_key and the field that changes.","Job clusters are matched by job_cluster_key and tasks by task_key, so the keys must be the same in both places.","Fields set in only one place are kept; fields set in both take the target's value. Here num_workers becomes 8 and spark_version and node type stay.","Run databricks bundle validate -t prod (or --output json) to see the merged result before deploying."],"answer":"In targets.prod.resources, repeat the job with the same job_cluster_key and set only num_workers: 8.","code":"targets:\n  prod:\n    resources:\n      jobs:\n        daily_sales:\n          job_clusters:\n            - job_cluster_key: main\n              new_cluster:\n                num_workers: 8"},{"t":"Why the dev job did not run on schedule","q":"You deployed with databricks bundle deploy (no -t). The job appears as \"[dev asha] daily_sales\", and its 6 am schedule never fires. A colleague's copy shows \"[dev ravi] daily_sales\". Explain.","steps":["Without -t, the CLI uses the target with default: true, here dev.","dev is in mode: development, which prefixes names with [dev user] so each developer gets a separate copy, and pauses all schedules and triggers so dev copies do not run on their own.","That is intended. To test the schedule, set the job's pause_status to UNPAUSED in the dev target, or run it with databricks bundle run.","Production deploys must say -t prod explicitly in CI, so nobody deploys to prod by accident."],"answer":"The default target is dev in development mode: names are prefixed per user and schedules are paused. Run it with bundle run, or unpause it in dev if you need to test the schedule.","code":"databricks bundle deploy            # uses the default target (dev)\ndatabricks bundle run daily_sales   # run the dev copy now\ndatabricks bundle deploy -t prod    # production, explicitly"},{"t":"Automating deploys","q":"Design the CI pipeline: every pull request should be checked, and every merge to main should deploy to prod. Production jobs must not run as a person.","steps":["On a pull request: install the Databricks CLI, run unit tests, then databricks bundle validate -t prod. Validation catches bad YAML and unresolved variables before anything is deployed.","Optionally run databricks bundle plan -t prod to show reviewers what would change.","On a merge to main: databricks bundle deploy -t prod. Authenticate the CI system as a service principal using a secret stored in the CI system, never in the repository.","In the prod target, set run_as to the service principal (service_principal_name is its application ID), so jobs run as it, not as whoever deployed.","Add permissions in the target so the team can view runs, and keep CAN MANAGE with the platform group.","If you want, add a test target between dev and prod and deploy there first, then run the job with bundle run as an integration test."],"answer":"PR: tests plus bundle validate (and plan) for prod. Merge: bundle deploy -t prod authenticated as a service principal, with run_as set to that service principal.","code":"# pull request\ndatabricks bundle validate -t prod\n\n# merge to main\ndatabricks bundle deploy -t prod\n\n# databricks.yml (prod target)\n    run_as:\n      service_principal_name: \"6a1b...\"\n    permissions:\n      - level: CAN_VIEW\n        group_name: data-engineers"},{"t":"Bring an existing job under the bundle","q":"A job was built by hand in the UI months ago and now must be managed by the bundle, without creating a second copy.","steps":["databricks bundle generate job --existing-job-id <id> writes YAML for the job (and downloads its notebooks) into the bundle.","Review the generated YAML, replace environment-specific values with variables, and commit it.","databricks bundle deployment bind links the bundle's job to the existing job, so the next deploy updates it rather than creating a duplicate.","From then on, change the job only through the bundle; edits made in the UI are overwritten at the next deploy."],"answer":"bundle generate job to create its YAML, then bundle deployment bind to link it to the existing job, then deploy as usual.","code":"databricks bundle generate job --existing-job-id 123456789\ndatabricks bundle deployment bind daily_sales 123456789 -t prod\ndatabricks bundle deploy -t prod"}],"mistakes":["Looking for a Create pull request button in the Git folder; pull requests are opened in the Git provider.","Using Reset on a shared branch; it force-pushes and rewrites the remote branch.","Copying the whole job into each target instead of using variables and small overrides.","Using bundle variables for values that change per run; they are fixed at deploy time, so use job parameters.","Deploying without -t and surprising yourself with the default target.","Expecting a development-mode deploy to run on schedule; development mode pauses schedules and triggers.","Deploying to prod as a person, or storing CI tokens in the repository.","Skipping bundle validate in CI, so a typo is found only during the production deploy.","Editing a bundle-managed job in the UI; the next deploy overwrites it."],"links":[{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/repos/git-operations-with-repos","t":"Create and manage Git folders","why":"Branch, Commit & Push, pull, merge, rebase, reset and conflicts."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/repos/ci-cd","t":"CI/CD with Databricks Git folders","why":"How Git folders fit into a CI/CD process."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/","t":"What are Declarative Automation Bundles?","why":"What a bundle is, what it can deploy, and the former name."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/settings","t":"Bundle configuration","why":"databricks.yml mappings: targets, run_as, permissions, include."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/variables","t":"Substitutions and variables","why":"Defining variables, lookups and their order of precedence."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/overrides","t":"Override with target settings","why":"How target resources merge with top-level ones."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/deployment-modes","t":"Bundle deployment modes","why":"Exactly what development and production modes change and check."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/cli/bundle-commands","t":"bundle command group","why":"Every bundle command and its flags."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/run-as","t":"Specify a run identity for a bundle workflow","why":"Running production jobs as a service principal."}],"checked":"Checked against the exam guide of 6 August 2026 and the Databricks docs (AWS) on 2 November 2026. Bundles were renamed Declarative Automation Bundles in the docs; the exam guide may still say Databricks Asset Bundles. Check CLI flags such as --existing-job-id against your CLI version in a free Databricks workspace."}},{"id":"tune","name":"Troubleshooting, monitoring and optimisation","w":10,"study":{"cert":"dbx-dea","dom":"tune","intro":"This section, 10% of the exam, is about reading what Databricks tells you when a job is slow or broken: the job's run history, the Spark UI, the compute event log. Then it asks for the right fix, which is often a table layout change (liquid clustering, predictive optimization) rather than more hardware. Work the solved problems with the answer hidden, then practise.","expects":["Spot performance trends by comparing a run with earlier runs in the job's run history.","Judge a job's health in the Jobs UI: statuses, blocked tasks in the graph, durations and failure rates.","Find skew, heavy shuffles and spill from stage metrics in the Spark UI.","Explain liquid clustering and predictive optimization, and when to use them.","Diagnose compute start-up failures, library conflicts and out-of-memory errors."],"ideas":[{"t":"Start from the job, then go down","b":"The Runs tab's matrix view shows each past run as a column: total duration as a bar, and one cell per task coloured by status. A bar that grows week after week is a trend; a column of red in one row is a task that keeps failing. Open a run's Timeline view to see which task took longest, then go to that task's Spark UI (classic) or query profile (serverless). Run history is kept for 60 days; the system.lakeflow tables keep it longer for trend queries."},{"t":"Read the statuses","b":"A run's result comes from its leaf tasks: Succeeded, Succeeded with failures, Failed, Timed Out, Canceled or Skipped. Skipped often means the job hit its concurrent run limit. Inside a run, Upstream failed means a task never started because something before it failed, so look for the first red task, not the last."},{"t":"Skew","b":"In a stage's Summary Metrics, compare the Max task duration with the 75th percentile. If Max is far above it (half as much again or more), a few tasks are doing most of the work, usually because a few keys hold most rows. Adaptive query execution splits skewed partitions in sort merge joins when they pass its thresholds; otherwise salt the hot key or pre-aggregate."},{"t":"Shuffle and spill","b":"A shuffle moves rows between executors for joins, aggregations and repartitions; large Shuffle Read and Write sizes mark the expensive stages. Spill means a task ran out of memory and wrote data to disk, which is slow. Fixes: spark.sql.shuffle.partitions = auto, read fewer rows and columns, broadcast a small side, or use workers with more memory."},{"t":"Liquid clustering","b":"CLUSTER BY (cols) organises a Delta table's files by up to four keys, chosen from the columns queries filter on most. It replaces partitioning and ZORDER (it cannot be combined with either), and keys can be changed later without rewriting old data. OPTIMIZE clusters new data incrementally; OPTIMIZE FULL reclusters everything after a key change. CLUSTER BY AUTO lets Databricks pick keys from query history on Unity Catalog managed tables with predictive optimization."},{"t":"Predictive optimization","b":"On Unity Catalog managed tables, Databricks decides when to run OPTIMIZE (compaction and liquid clustering), VACUUM and ANALYZE, on serverless compute billed to you. It is on by default for accounts created since November 2024 and is being turned on for older ones. Enable or disable it per catalog, schema or table with ALTER ... ENABLE / DISABLE / INHERIT PREDICTIVE OPTIMIZATION. It does nothing for external tables."},{"t":"Compute that will not start","b":"The compute event log shows a termination code. Cloud capacity or quota errors: try another instance type or zone, or ask for more quota. Network errors: firewall, DNS or routes block the control plane or storage. INIT_SCRIPT_FAILURE: read the init script logs (cluster log delivery) and fix the script. Serverless avoids most of these, because Databricks manages the machines."},{"t":"Libraries and memory","b":"%pip install in a notebook affects only that notebook's session; run dbutils.library.restartPython() after installing so the new version loads, and pin versions with == so runs are repeatable. Clashes usually come from an unpinned package pulling a different version of something the runtime already has. Out of memory on the driver usually means collect() or toPandas() on large data; on executors it usually means skew, too few shuffle partitions, an oversized broadcast or a heavy UDF."}],"tables":[{"title":"Symptom to cause (our summary of the docs)","head":["What you see","Where","Likely cause","First fix"],"rows":[["Run time grows a little every week","Runs tab matrix, duration bars","Data growth, small files, no clustering","Enable predictive optimization; liquid clustering on filter columns"],["Max task time far above the 75th percentile","Spark UI stage, Summary Metrics","Skew on a few keys","Check AQE skew join; salt or pre-aggregate the hot key"],["Spill (memory/disk) in stage details","Spark UI stage page","Partitions too large for memory","shuffle.partitions = auto; less data; more memory per core"],["Huge shuffle read/write","Spark UI stages","Join or aggregation on large data","Broadcast the small side; filter and select columns early"],["ExecutorLostFailure, executors removed","Spark UI, event log","Executor out of memory","Rule out spot loss; fix skew or UDFs; more memory per core"],["Driver out of memory","Driver logs","collect() or toPandas() on large data","Keep work distributed; write results to a table"],["Compute terminated before the job started","Compute event log","Capacity, quota, network or init script","Act on the termination code"],["ImportError or an old version after %pip install","Notebook output","Python not restarted, or a version clash","restartPython(); pin versions"],["Task shows Upstream failed","Job run graph","An earlier task failed","Fix the first failed task, then Repair run"]]}],"problems":[{"t":"A job that is slowly getting slower","q":"A nightly job took 25 minutes in August and takes 70 minutes now. Nothing has failed. How do you find out why?","steps":["Open the job's Runs tab. In the matrix view, the duration bars show when it started growing; the task cells show which task's colour or duration changed.","Open a recent slow run and a fast older run side by side. Compare rows read and written: if input grew three times, the time growth may simply follow the data.","In the slow run's Timeline view, find the task that grew. Go to its query profile (serverless) or Spark UI (classic).","Look for a scan that reads far more files than it returns rows (no data skipping), many tiny files, or a stage with skew or spill.","For history beyond 60 days or across many jobs, query the jobs system tables in system.lakeflow.","Fix the cause (often table layout), then add a duration Warning so a future slowdown sends a notification."],"answer":"Use the matrix view to find when and which task grew, compare a slow and a fast run's rows and plan, drill into the slow task's Spark UI or query profile, fix the cause, and set a duration warning.","code":null},{"t":"Which task really failed?","q":"A run shows Failed. In the graph, ingest succeeded, clean failed, and three later tasks show Upstream failed. A notify task with Run if All done succeeded. Which task do you fix, and what is the run's result based on?","steps":["Upstream failed means those tasks never started; they are not the problem.","clean is the first task that failed. Open it for its error message and output.","A run's result comes from its leaf tasks (tasks with nothing after them). Here the leaf tasks include ones marked Upstream failed, so the run is Failed even though notify succeeded.","After fixing clean, use Repair run: it reruns clean and the three tasks after it, not ingest."],"answer":"Fix clean, the first failed task, then Repair run. The run is Failed because its leaf tasks did not succeed.","code":null},{"t":"Skew in a join","q":"A join stage has 400 tasks. Summary Metrics: 75th percentile duration 12 s, Max 18 min. The longest tasks read 9 GB of shuffle data each; the rest read about 50 MB. What is happening and what do you do?","steps":["Max far above the 75th percentile, with a few tasks reading most of the shuffle data, is skew: a few join keys hold most rows (for example a null or a default customer ID).","Check whether the hot key is junk. Filtering nulls or a placeholder key before the join often removes the skew completely.","Check that adaptive query execution is on. It splits skewed partitions in sort merge joins, but only when they pass its size thresholds, which can be tuned.","If the key is real and hot, salt it: add a random number 0 to N to the key on the big side, copy the small side N times, and join on both.","If the source table's layout causes the skew, liquid clustering on the join key can help."],"answer":"Skew. Remove junk hot keys first, rely on AQE skew join (tune its thresholds if needed), and salt the key if it is genuinely hot.","code":"from pyspark.sql import functions as F\nN = 16\nbig = orders.withColumn(\"salt\", (F.rand() * N).cast(\"int\"))\nsmall = customers.crossJoin(spark.range(N).withColumnRenamed(\"id\", \"salt\"))\njoined = big.join(small, [\"customer_id\", \"salt\"])"},{"t":"Spill on an aggregation","q":"An aggregation stage shows 120 GB spilled to disk across its 200 tasks, with no skew in Summary Metrics. The cluster has 8 workers with 4 cores and 16 GB each. Fix it.","steps":["No skew means every partition is simply too big for the memory each task gets. 200 shuffle partitions is Spark's default, too few for this data.","Set spark.sql.shuffle.partitions to auto so Databricks sizes partitions from the data.","Reduce what goes into the shuffle: filter rows and select only needed columns before the aggregation.","If spill remains, use workers with more memory per core (for example 4 cores and 32 GB). Doubling memory per core is also the quick test for a memory problem.","Re-run and compare spill and duration; keep the change only if both improve."],"answer":"Set shuffle partitions to auto, shrink the data before the shuffle, and if needed move to more memory per core.","code":"spark.conf.set(\"spark.sql.shuffle.partitions\", \"auto\")"},{"t":"Partitioned table, slow filters","q":"main.silver.events is partitioned by event_date and Z-ordered by customer_id. Analysts mostly filter on customer_id and region; the table has hundreds of thousands of small files. The table is managed. What layout do you move to?","steps":["Liquid clustering is recommended for new tables and replaces both partitioning and ZORDER. It cannot be combined with either.","Choose keys from the columns queries filter on: customer_id and region (up to four keys). Order does not matter.","On Runtime 18.1 and later, ALTER TABLE ... REPLACE PARTITIONED BY WITH CLUSTER BY converts the table in place; otherwise create a clustered table and copy the data (CTAS).","Run OPTIMIZE FULL once so existing data is reclustered. After that, OPTIMIZE clusters new data incrementally.","As a managed table it can use predictive optimization, which runs OPTIMIZE for you. Or set CLUSTER BY AUTO and let Databricks choose keys from query history."],"answer":"Move to liquid clustering on (customer_id, region), recluster once with OPTIMIZE FULL, and let predictive optimization keep it clustered (or use CLUSTER BY AUTO).","code":"CREATE TABLE main.silver.events_c\nCLUSTER BY (customer_id, region)\nAS SELECT * FROM main.silver.events;\n\n-- later, if query patterns change\nALTER TABLE main.silver.events_c CLUSTER BY (customer_id, event_date);\nOPTIMIZE main.silver.events_c FULL;"},{"t":"Who runs OPTIMIZE and VACUUM?","q":"A team has 40 nightly OPTIMIZE and VACUUM jobs for its tables. Half the tables are managed, half external. Can predictive optimization replace these jobs?","steps":["Predictive optimization works only on Unity Catalog managed tables. It decides when to run OPTIMIZE, VACUUM and ANALYZE and runs them on serverless compute.","For the managed tables, check that it is enabled (DESCRIBE CATALOG/SCHEMA EXTENDED, or ALTER ... ENABLE PREDICTIVE OPTIMIZATION), then retire their jobs.","External tables are not covered, so keep their jobs, or convert the tables to managed with SET MANAGED.","See what it did, and what it cost, in system.storage.predictive_optimization_operations_history.","VACUUM keeps files for 7 days by default (delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration), which also limits how far back time travel works."],"answer":"Yes for the managed tables (enable predictive optimization and drop their jobs); no for external tables, unless converted to managed.","code":"ALTER SCHEMA main.silver ENABLE PREDICTIVE OPTIMIZATION;\n\nSELECT * FROM system.storage.predictive_optimization_operations_history\nWHERE table_name = 'orders'\nORDER BY start_time DESC;"},{"t":"Compute that never starts","q":"A job on classic jobs compute fails before any task runs. Last week it failed with INIT_SCRIPT_FAILURE; today it fails with an insufficient-capacity error for the instance type. What do you do for each?","steps":["Start in the compute event log: the termination code names the cause.","INIT_SCRIPT_FAILURE: the cluster-scoped init script failed. Turn on cluster log delivery and read the init script's stderr log; fix the path, permissions or failing command. A global init script failure needs a workspace admin.","Insufficient capacity: the cloud has no machines of that type in that zone right now. Choose another instance type or zone, enable flexible node types, or retry later; a quota error needs a quota increase.","Network errors (DNS, firewall, routes to the control plane or storage) are fixed by the network team, not by changing Spark settings.","Where possible, move the job to serverless compute, which removes instance choice, init scripts and capacity planning from your hands."],"answer":"Read the termination code in the event log. Init script: read its logs and fix the script. Capacity: change instance type or zone, or retry. Consider serverless to avoid both.","code":null},{"t":"A library that will not behave","q":"A notebook runs %pip install pandas==2.2.3 in its last cell, then imports it in another cell and still gets the old version. On another day an unpinned %pip install of a package breaks a library that came with the runtime.","steps":["%pip does not restart Python. Call dbutils.library.restartPython() after installing, and put installs in the first cells so the rest of the notebook sees them. Restarting clears Python variables, so do it before real work.","Notebook-scoped installs affect only this notebook's session. Other notebooks on the same cluster keep their versions.","Unpinned installs pull the latest versions, which may clash with the runtime's own packages. Pin versions with ==, or keep a requirements.txt and install from it.","If a cluster library and a notebook-scoped library clash, %pip uninstall in the notebook returns to the runtime or cluster version.","On serverless, set dependencies in the notebook's Environment panel so every run gets the same set."],"answer":"Install first, then dbutils.library.restartPython(); pin versions or use a requirements file; on serverless use the Environment panel.","code":"%pip install -r /Workspace/Shared/etl/requirements.txt\n\ndbutils.library.restartPython()"},{"t":"Out of memory: driver or executor?","q":"(a) A notebook calls df.toPandas() on a 200 GB table and fails. (b) A job fails with ExecutorLostFailure after executors are removed repeatedly during a join; the cluster uses spot instances. Diagnose both.","steps":["(a) toPandas() and collect() bring every row to the driver. 200 GB cannot fit. Aggregate or sample in Spark first, or write the result to a table. More workers do not help the driver.","(b) First rule out spot instances being taken back: the compute event log shows instance losses. That is not a memory problem.","If the losses are not spot reclaims, executors are running out of memory. Check the stage for skew, too few shuffle partitions, a large broadcast or a Python UDF that loads a whole partition.","Test by doubling memory per core with the same core count. If the job lasts longer or succeeds, memory is the cause; then fix the root cause rather than keeping the bigger machines."],"answer":"(a) Driver OOM from toPandas; keep work in Spark. (b) Check the event log for spot losses first; otherwise executor OOM, so look for skew, partitions, broadcasts and UDFs.","code":null}],"mistakes":["Fixing the last red task in a run instead of the first one; Upstream failed tasks never ran.","Adding workers to fix a driver out-of-memory error from collect() or toPandas().","Raising memory for every slow job without checking for skew first.","Combining liquid clustering with partitioning or ZORDER, which is not allowed.","Choosing more than four clustering keys, or keys queries never filter on.","Changing clustering keys and expecting old data to recluster without OPTIMIZE FULL.","Expecting predictive optimization to maintain external tables.","Forgetting dbutils.library.restartPython() after %pip install, or leaving versions unpinned.","Treating spot instance losses as out-of-memory errors."],"links":[{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/monitor","t":"Monitor Lakeflow Jobs","why":"The Runs tab, matrix and list views, statuses and filters."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/jobs/diagnose-job-performance","t":"Diagnose Lakeflow Jobs performance","why":"Comparing runs and moving from a task to its queries or Spark UI."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/system-tables/jobs","t":"Jobs system table reference","why":"Run history across jobs for trends and failure rates."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/spark-ui-guide","t":"Diagnose cost and performance issues using the Spark UI","why":"Where to start in the Spark UI."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/spark-ui-guide/long-spark-stage-page","t":"Skew and spill","why":"How to spot each in a stage and what to do about it."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/clustering","t":"Use liquid clustering for tables","why":"CLUSTER BY, AUTO, OPTIMIZE FULL, key limits and conversions."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/predictive-optimization","t":"Predictive optimization","why":"What it runs, which tables, how to enable it and how to monitor it."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/libraries/notebooks-python-libraries","t":"Notebook-scoped Python libraries","why":"%pip scope, restarting Python and pinning versions."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/compute/troubleshooting/cluster-error-codes","t":"Classic compute termination error codes","why":"What each start-up failure code means and how to fix it."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/optimizations/spark-ui-guide/spark-memory-issues","t":"Spark memory issues","why":"Recognising and fixing executor out-of-memory errors."}],"checked":"Checked against the exam guide of 6 August 2026 and the Databricks docs on 2 November 2026 (AWS, and GCP for the three pages marked as such). Driver out-of-memory advice and the salting pattern come from general Spark practice; check them in a free Databricks workspace."}},{"id":"gov","name":"Governance and security","w":15,"study":{"cert":"dbx-dea","dom":"gov","intro":"Governance is 15% of the exam and it is all Unity Catalog: which table type owns its files, who can do what at which level, and how to hide rows and columns from people who should not see them. The rules are few but exact, so learn the privileges by name. Work the solved problems with the answer hidden, then practise.","expects":["Tell managed tables from external tables, and create, change, drop and convert them.","Grant and revoke privileges at the right level (catalog, schema or table) for users, groups and service principals, in SQL or in Catalog Explorer.","Know where denying access fits in Unity Catalog, which has no DENY statement.","Hide rows with row filters and values with column masks, based on group membership.","Use attribute-based access control (ABAC) policies with governed tags to filter and mask centrally across many tables."],"ideas":[{"t":"Three levels, one name","b":"Every table is catalog.schema.table inside a metastore. Catalogs and schemas are containers; tables, views, volumes and functions live inside them. Layers such as bronze, silver and gold are usually schemas or catalogs."},{"t":"Managed or external: who owns the files","b":"Unity Catalog manages a managed table's files in the catalog's or schema's storage, optimises them (predictive optimization) and deletes them after DROP TABLE, once the recovery period ends (7 days by default; UNDROP brings a table back inside it). An external table points at a path under an external location; DROP TABLE removes only the definition and leaves the files. Databricks recommends managed tables unless other systems must read the files directly or the format is not Delta or Iceberg."},{"t":"Converting external to managed","b":"ALTER TABLE ... SET MANAGED copies the data and log into managed storage, with only a minute or two of blocked writes at the end. It needs a Delta table, table ownership, and Databricks Runtime 17.3 LTS or serverless. The old files are kept for 14 days, and UNSET MANAGED rolls back within that window."},{"t":"Privileges flow down","b":"A privilege on a catalog applies to every schema and table in it, including future ones; a privilege on a schema applies to its tables. To read a table a user needs three things: USE CATALOG on the catalog, USE SCHEMA on the schema and SELECT on the table (or on a parent). MODIFY allows writes, CREATE TABLE allows new tables in a schema, and BROWSE lets people see that objects exist without reading them."},{"t":"Who can grant","b":"The object's owner (the creator, at first), the owner of its catalog or schema, a metastore admin, or anyone holding MANAGE on the object. MANAGE lets someone grant, revoke and drop without owning the object, and several principals can hold it. Grant to groups rather than people, so access follows team changes."},{"t":"There is no DENY statement","b":"Unity Catalog grants are additive: you remove access with REVOKE, and a user keeps whatever another grant or group still gives them. The legacy Hive metastore table ACLs had DENY; Unity Catalog does not. The only deny in Unity Catalog is the ABAC DENY policy (Beta), which today covers only the MANAGE ACCESS CONTROL privilege and overrides every grant."},{"t":"Row filters and column masks","b":"Both are SQL functions attached to one table. A row filter returns true for rows a user may see; a column mask returns the real value or a masked one. Inside them, is_account_group_member('group') checks who is asking. The table owner or a holder of MANAGE attaches them. One mask per column, and filters and masks cannot be put on a view."},{"t":"ABAC: one policy, many tables","b":"Governed tags are defined once for the account (for example pii = ssn). Tag columns and tables, then write a policy on a catalog or schema: TO which principals, EXCEPT whom, which tagged columns to mask or which rows to filter. New tables that get the tag are covered automatically, and table owners cannot override the policy. Needs Databricks Runtime 16.4 or serverless."}],"tables":[{"title":"Managed and external tables (our summary of the docs)","head":["","Managed","External"],"rows":[["Files live in","The catalog's or schema's managed storage","A path you choose under an external location"],["Create with","CREATE TABLE c.s.t (...)","CREATE TABLE c.s.t (...) LOCATION 's3://...'"],["Privileges to create","USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA, CREATE TABLE","The same, plus CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE on the external location"],["DROP TABLE","Deletes the files after the recovery period; UNDROP within it","Removes the definition only; files stay"],["Formats","Delta Lake or Iceberg","Delta, Parquet, CSV, JSON, Avro, ORC, text"],["Optimisation","Predictive optimization can run OPTIMIZE and VACUUM for you","Yours to run"],["Change type","UNSET MANAGED (only to roll back a SET MANAGED within 14 days)","ALTER TABLE ... SET MANAGED"]]},{"title":"Privileges you will meet most","head":["Privilege","On","Allows"],"rows":[["USE CATALOG / USE SCHEMA","Catalog / schema","Reaching objects inside; needed alongside any object privilege"],["SELECT","Table, view, or a parent","Reading"],["MODIFY","Table, or a parent","INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE"],["CREATE TABLE","Schema, or catalog","Creating tables in that schema"],["CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE","External location","Creating external tables under that path"],["READ VOLUME / WRITE VOLUME","Volume, or a parent","Reading or writing files in a volume"],["BROWSE","Catalog","Seeing names and metadata, and requesting access, without reading data"],["MANAGE","Any securable","Granting, revoking, transferring ownership and dropping, without owning it"],["ALL PRIVILEGES","Any securable","Every privilege except MANAGE"]]},{"title":"Three ways to limit what people see","head":["Option","Scope","Managed by","Good for"],"rows":[["Dynamic view","One view over one or more tables","The view owner","Sharing a curated slice; readers query the view, not the table"],["Row filter / column mask","One table, attached with ALTER TABLE","The table owner or MANAGE holder","A few tables with their own rules"],["ABAC policy","A catalog, schema or table, matched by governed tags","Holders of MANAGE on the catalog or schema","The same rule across many tables, including new ones"]]}],"problems":[{"t":"Pick the table type","q":"(a) The data team builds silver and gold tables only Databricks uses. (b) A partner's Spark cluster outside Databricks must read the same Parquet files the team loads, and dropping the table must never delete them. Which table type for each, and what privileges does (b) need?","steps":["(a) Nothing outside needs the files, so use managed tables: Unity Catalog places them, predictive optimization can compact and vacuum them, and DROP TABLE cleans up.","(b) Files must stay at a known path and survive a drop, so use an external table with LOCATION under a registered external location.","For (b) you need USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA and CREATE TABLE on the schema, plus CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE on the external location that covers the path.","Remember that Unity Catalog privileges do not apply when the partner reads the files straight from storage; storage permissions protect them there."],"answer":"(a) Managed tables. (b) An external table with LOCATION; it needs CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE on the external location as well as the usual schema privileges.","code":"-- (a)\nCREATE TABLE main.silver.orders (order_id STRING, amount DECIMAL(12,2));\n\n-- (b)\nCREATE TABLE main.shared.partner_orders (order_id STRING, amount DECIMAL(12,2))\nUSING PARQUET\nLOCATION 's3://acme-shared/partner_orders/';"},{"t":"What a drop really deletes","q":"An engineer runs DROP TABLE on main.silver.orders (managed) and on main.shared.partner_orders (external) by mistake. What is lost, and how do you recover each?","steps":["The managed table's files are deleted only after the recovery period, 7 days by default. Within that window, UNDROP TABLE brings the table back.","The recovery period can be set to 0 (no recovery) or 7 to 30 days on a catalog or schema, so check the setting before relying on it.","The external table's files are untouched. Only the definition is gone, so create the table again with the same LOCATION and the data reappears.","Grants on the dropped external table are gone with the definition, so they need to be applied again."],"answer":"Managed: UNDROP TABLE within the recovery period. External: the files are safe; recreate the table on the same LOCATION and re-grant.","code":"UNDROP TABLE main.silver.orders;\n\nCREATE TABLE main.shared.partner_orders\nUSING PARQUET\nLOCATION 's3://acme-shared/partner_orders/';"},{"t":"Convert an external table to managed","q":"main.legacy.events is an external Delta table (3 TB). The team now wants predictive optimization and no longer needs outside readers. Convert it safely.","steps":["Check the conditions: it must be Delta, you must own the table, and the conversion must run on Databricks Runtime 17.3 LTS or serverless.","Cancel running OPTIMIZE jobs on the table and pause any that are scheduled during the conversion.","Run ALTER TABLE ... SET MANAGED. Data and log are copied first with no downtime; then writes are blocked for a minute or two while the table is repointed. Readers on Runtime 16.4 LTS or later see no downtime.","If something breaks, ALTER TABLE ... UNSET MANAGED rolls back within 14 days, and writes made since conversion are kept.","After 14 days the old files are removed (by predictive optimization, or run VACUUM yourself). The table name, history and grants stay the same throughout."],"answer":"Own the table, use Runtime 17.3 LTS or serverless, pause OPTIMIZE, run ALTER TABLE main.legacy.events SET MANAGED, and keep UNSET MANAGED as a 14-day rollback.","code":"ALTER TABLE main.legacy.events SET MANAGED;\n\n-- roll back within 14 days if needed\nALTER TABLE main.legacy.events UNSET MANAGED;"},{"t":"A permission error despite SELECT","q":"The analysts group has SELECT on main.sales.orders, yet every query fails with a permission error. Fix it with the least access.","steps":["Reading a table needs USE CATALOG on main and USE SCHEMA on main.sales as well as SELECT. SELECT alone is never enough.","Grant the two usage privileges to the group. They let the group reach objects; they do not give data access by themselves.","Check with SHOW GRANTS that the group, not a single person, holds the privileges.","Do not grant ALL PRIVILEGES or metastore admin to solve a missing USE privilege."],"answer":"GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG main and GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA main.sales to the analysts group.","code":"GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG main TO `analysts`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA main.sales TO `analysts`;\nSHOW GRANTS `analysts` ON TABLE main.sales.orders;"},{"t":"Grant at the right level","q":"(a) The BI group must read every current and future table in main.gold. (b) The ingest job's service principal (application ID 7f3c...e21) must write to main.bronze.orders only. (c) Priya should manage access to main.gold without owning it. Write the grants.","steps":["(a) SELECT on the schema is inherited by every table in it, including tables created later. Add USE SCHEMA, and USE CATALOG on main. Table names cannot use wildcards.","(b) Grant on the single table: SELECT and MODIFY, plus the usage privileges. A service principal is named by its application ID in backticks.","(c) MANAGE lets Priya grant, revoke and drop without ownership. It does not give her data access by itself.","Users are named by email in backticks; groups by group name. Prefer groups for people."],"answer":"(a) USE CATALOG on main, USE SCHEMA and SELECT on main.gold to the BI group. (b) USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA on main.bronze, SELECT and MODIFY on main.bronze.orders to the service principal's application ID. (c) MANAGE on the schema to Priya.","code":"GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG main TO `bi_team`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA, SELECT ON SCHEMA main.gold TO `bi_team`;\n\nGRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG main TO `7f3c...e21`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA main.bronze TO `7f3c...e21`;\nGRANT SELECT, MODIFY ON TABLE main.bronze.orders TO `7f3c...e21`;\n\nGRANT MANAGE ON SCHEMA main.gold TO `priya@acme.com`;"},{"t":"Taking access away","q":"Contractors must not read main.gold.salaries. They are in the contractors group, which has SELECT on the schema main.gold. Someone suggests DENY SELECT. What do you do?","steps":["Unity Catalog has no DENY statement. DENY belonged to the legacy Hive metastore table ACLs.","Grants add up, so you cannot carve one table out of a schema-level grant. Revoke the schema grant and grant SELECT only on the tables contractors may read.","Or move salaries to a separate schema that contractors have no privileges on, which keeps the schema-level grant simple.","Check that no other group a contractor belongs to still grants access: use SHOW GRANTS on the table.","If only some columns are sensitive, a column mask or an ABAC policy can hide them instead of removing the whole table."],"answer":"Revoke SELECT on the schema from contractors and grant table-level SELECT only where allowed (or move salaries to its own schema). There is no DENY to use.","code":"REVOKE SELECT ON SCHEMA main.gold FROM `contractors`;\nGRANT SELECT ON TABLE main.gold.daily_revenue TO `contractors`;\nSHOW GRANTS ON TABLE main.gold.salaries;"},{"t":"A row filter by region","q":"main.sales.orders holds every region. Members of the group sales_IN should see only India rows, sales_US only US rows; the admins group sees everything. Implement it on the table.","steps":["Write a SQL function that returns true for rows the caller may see. is_account_group_member checks the caller's groups.","Return true for admins first, then compare the row's region with the caller's region groups.","Attach it with ALTER TABLE ... SET ROW FILTER ... ON (region); the function's parameter type must match the column.","The table owner or a MANAGE holder attaches it, and every query on the table applies it, including MERGE.","Remove the filter from the table before dropping the function, or the table becomes unreadable."],"answer":"A function returning is_account_group_member('admins') OR is_account_group_member(concat('sales_', region)), attached with SET ROW FILTER ON (region).","code":"CREATE FUNCTION main.sec.region_rows(region STRING)\nRETURN is_account_group_member('admins')\n    OR is_account_group_member(concat('sales_', region));\n\nALTER TABLE main.sales.orders SET ROW FILTER main.sec.region_rows ON (region);"},{"t":"Mask a column","q":"main.hr.employees has a pan column. Only the hr_payroll group may see full values; everyone else sees only the last four characters.","steps":["Write a mask function that takes the column value and returns either the value or a masked version of the same type.","Use is_account_group_member('hr_payroll') to decide. Everyone else gets a fixed prefix plus right(pan, 4).","Attach it with ALTER TABLE ... ALTER COLUMN pan SET MASK. Each column can have one mask.","Masks and filters stop some features on that table, such as time travel and clones, so check before applying them to tables that use those."],"answer":"A mask function returning pan for hr_payroll and concat('XXXXXX', right(pan, 4)) for others, set on the pan column.","code":"CREATE FUNCTION main.sec.pan_mask(pan STRING)\nRETURN CASE WHEN is_account_group_member('hr_payroll') THEN pan\n            ELSE concat('XXXXXX', right(pan, 4)) END;\n\nALTER TABLE main.hr.employees ALTER COLUMN pan SET MASK main.sec.pan_mask;"},{"t":"One policy for every PII column","q":"The security team wants every column tagged pii = email, in any table of the catalog main, masked for all users except the privacy group. New tables are added weekly. Table-by-table masks keep getting missed.","steps":["Create the governed tag pii with allowed values (such as email) once, at account level, in Catalog > Govern > Governed Tags.","Tag the columns: ALTER TABLE ... ALTER COLUMN email SET TAGS ('pii' = 'email'). Column tags are not inherited from the table, so each column is tagged directly. Tagging needs ASSIGN on the tag and APPLY TAG on the object.","Write the mask function once.","Create a COLUMN MASK policy ON CATALOG main, TO `account users` EXCEPT `privacy`, FOR TABLES, MATCH COLUMNS has_tag_value('pii', 'email').","Every tagged column in the catalog is masked, including new tables once their columns are tagged. Table owners cannot override it. Creating the policy needs MANAGE on the catalog and EXECUTE on the function.","Readers need Databricks Runtime 16.4 or serverless; older runtimes cannot read ABAC-secured tables."],"answer":"A governed tag pii, tagged email columns, and one CREATE POLICY ... COLUMN MASK on the catalog for account users except privacy, matching has_tag_value('pii', 'email').","code":"ALTER TABLE main.crm.customers ALTER COLUMN email SET TAGS ('pii' = 'email');\n\nCREATE FUNCTION main.sec.hide_email(email STRING) RETURNS STRING\nRETURN '***@***';\n\nCREATE POLICY mask_emails\nON CATALOG main\nCOLUMN MASK main.sec.hide_email\nTO `account users` EXCEPT `privacy`\nFOR TABLES\nMATCH COLUMNS has_tag_value('pii', 'email') AS e\nON COLUMN e;"}],"mistakes":["Granting SELECT on a table and forgetting USE CATALOG and USE SCHEMA.","Trying GRANT SELECT ON TABLE main.sales.*; there are no wildcards, grant on the schema instead.","Writing DENY SELECT. Unity Catalog removes access with REVOKE; it has no DENY statement.","Expecting DROP TABLE on an external table to delete the files, or on a managed table to keep them.","Creating an external table without CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE on the external location.","Converting to managed with MOVE or COPY; those options are for foreign tables, and an external table uses plain SET MANAGED.","Granting to individual people instead of groups, so access drifts as teams change.","Dropping a mask or filter function before removing it from the table.","Putting a row filter or column mask on a view; they go on tables, and views use dynamic logic instead.","Tagging a table and expecting its columns to inherit the tag in MATCH COLUMNS; column tags are set column by column."],"links":[{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/managed","t":"Unity Catalog managed tables","why":"Storage, predictive optimization, the recovery period and UNDROP."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/external","t":"Work with external tables","why":"LOCATION, the privileges needed and what DROP TABLE leaves behind."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/convert-to-managed","t":"Convert external tables to managed","why":"SET MANAGED, its requirements, downtime and the 14-day rollback."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/access-control/permissions-concepts","t":"Unity Catalog permissions model","why":"Inheritance, ownership, MANAGE and BROWSE."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/access-control/privileges-reference","t":"Unity Catalog privileges reference","why":"Every privilege, what it allows and where it can be granted."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/manage-privileges","t":"Manage privileges in Unity Catalog","why":"GRANT, REVOKE and SHOW GRANTS, in SQL and Catalog Explorer."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/filters-and-masks/manually-apply","t":"Manually apply row filters and column masks","why":"The SQL to create, attach and remove filters and masks."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/filters-and-masks/","t":"Row filters and column masks","why":"How they work, their limits and the compute they need."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/abac/core-concepts","t":"Core concepts for ABAC","why":"Governed tags, policies, inheritance and DENY policies."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/abac/policies","t":"Create and manage ABAC policies","why":"CREATE POLICY syntax, who can create one and the limits."},{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/views/dynamic","t":"Create a dynamic view","why":"Filtering and masking inside a view definition."}],"checked":"Checked against the exam guide of 6 August 2026 and the Databricks docs (AWS) on 2 November 2026. The guide's wording mentions deny; the docs show no DENY statement in Unity Catalog, only ABAC DENY policies in Beta. ABAC and SET MANAGED are recent features; check them in a free Databricks workspace."}}],"questions":[{"id":"dbx-001","d":"platform","q":"What gives a Delta Lake table ACID transactions on top of files in cloud storage?","opts":["A transaction log of ordered commits stored next to the data files","A separate relational database that holds every row","Locking each Parquet file while it is being read","Writing every table as a single large file"],"ans":[0],"why":"Delta Lake records every change as a commit in the _delta_log folder. Readers use the log to see a consistent snapshot and writers add new commits, so changes are atomic and isolated even though the data itself is ordinary Parquet files."},{"id":"dbx-002","d":"platform","q":"A team needs compute for a nightly scheduled pipeline and wants the lowest cost with no idle time. Which choice fits best?","opts":["An all-purpose cluster left running all day","Job compute (or serverless jobs compute) that starts for the run and stops when it finishes","A SQL warehouse sized extra large","The driver node of a teammate's interactive cluster"],"ans":[1],"why":"Job compute exists only for the run and is billed at a lower rate than all-purpose compute, which is meant for interactive work. Serverless jobs compute removes start-up management too. Leaving an all-purpose cluster running wastes money while idle."},{"id":"dbx-003","d":"platform","q":"Analysts run SQL dashboards all day with many concurrent users. Which compute is designed for this?","opts":["A SQL warehouse","Single-node job compute","A notebook's local Python process","An all-purpose cluster with one worker"],"ans":[0],"why":"SQL warehouses are built for SQL and BI workloads: they scale for concurrent queries, and serverless warehouses start in seconds. Job and all-purpose clusters can run SQL but are not designed for many concurrent BI users."},{"id":"dbx-004","d":"platform","q":"In Unity Catalog, what is the order of the three-level namespace used to name a table?","opts":["schema.catalog.table","catalog.schema.table","metastore.table.column","workspace.catalog.table"],"ans":[1],"why":"Tables are addressed as catalog.schema.table, for example main.sales.orders. The metastore sits above catalogs, and the same three-level name works in every workspace attached to that metastore."},{"id":"dbx-129","d":"platform","q":"Where does code run when a job uses serverless compute?","opts":["In the serverless compute plane in Databricks' account, in the workspace's region","On virtual machines in your own cloud account and network","In the control plane next to the web application","On the user's laptop through the browser"],"ans":[0],"why":"Serverless compute runs in a compute plane that Databricks operates in its own account, in the same region as the workspace. Classic compute runs as virtual machines in your own cloud account. Table data stays in cloud object storage in both cases.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/getting-started/high-level-architecture","t":"High-level architecture"}},{"id":"dbx-130","d":"platform","q":"A team keeps an all-purpose cluster running all day so its scheduled jobs start quickly. What is the main cost problem?","opts":["All-purpose compute is charged while idle and at a higher rate than jobs or serverless compute","All-purpose compute cannot run scheduled jobs at all","Jobs on all-purpose compute are charged twice","All-purpose compute cannot use Delta tables"],"ans":[0],"why":"All-purpose compute charges for every minute it is up, including idle time, and costs more per DBU than jobs compute. Serverless jobs start in seconds and charge only while running, so there is no reason to keep a cluster warm.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/choose-compute","t":"Compute selection recommendations"}},{"id":"dbx-131","d":"platform","q":"A data scientist needs to work interactively in R. Which compute fits?","opts":["Classic all-purpose compute in Dedicated access mode","Serverless compute for notebooks","A serverless SQL warehouse","Classic compute in Standard access mode"],"ans":[0],"why":"R is supported on Dedicated access mode (formerly single user), not on Standard access mode or serverless. A SQL warehouse runs only SQL. Set auto termination so the cluster stops when idle.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/configure","t":"Compute configuration reference"}},{"id":"dbx-132","d":"platform","q":"Which statements about SQL warehouse types are true? (Choose two.)","opts":["Serverless SQL warehouses usually start in a few seconds","Pro is a good choice when serverless is not available in the region or custom networking is needed","Classic SQL warehouses have intelligent workload management","Pro SQL warehouses run in Databricks' account"],"ans":[0,1],"why":"Serverless warehouses start in about 2 to 6 seconds and have intelligent workload management. Pro and Classic run in your own cloud account and take minutes to start; Pro suits regions without serverless or setups needing custom networking.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/sql-warehouse/warehouse-types","t":"SQL warehouse types"}},{"id":"dbx-133","d":"platform","q":"A bad load overwrote rows in a Delta table an hour ago. What Delta Lake feature undoes it without reloading from source?","opts":["RESTORE TABLE to the version before the load, found with DESCRIBE HISTORY","VACUUM the table","OPTIMIZE the table","Drop and recreate the table from the latest files"],"ans":[0],"why":"Every Delta commit is a version. DESCRIBE HISTORY lists them and RESTORE TABLE ... TO VERSION AS OF returns the table to an earlier one, as long as VACUUM has not removed the older files.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/history","t":"Work with table history"}},{"id":"dbx-134","d":"platform","q":"Which part of the Databricks platform records who can use each table and keeps lineage across workspaces?","opts":["Unity Catalog","Delta Lake's transaction log","The Spark driver","The job scheduler"],"ans":[0],"why":"Unity Catalog is the governance layer: one metastore of catalogs, schemas and objects shared by attached workspaces, with privileges, lineage and audit logs. Delta Lake's log tracks a table's versions, not who may access it.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/getting-started/concepts","t":"Databricks components"}},{"id":"dbx-010","d":"ingest","q":"A COPY INTO command that loaded 500 files yesterday runs again today. Twenty new files have arrived in the same folder. What happens?","opts":["All 520 files are loaded again, creating duplicates","Only the 20 new files are loaded, because COPY INTO skips files it has already loaded","The command fails because the target table is not empty","It loads only files modified in the last hour"],"ans":[1],"why":"COPY INTO keeps track of the files it has loaded into a table and skips them on later runs, so rerunning it is safe and loads only new files. That idempotency is why it suits scheduled incremental loads of a few thousand files."},{"id":"dbx-011","d":"ingest","q":"Millions of small JSON files land in cloud storage every day and must be loaded incrementally with minimal setup. Which option is the best fit?","opts":["Auto Loader (the cloudFiles source)","A daily full read of the folder with spark.read.json","COPY INTO with a list of every file name","Manually uploading files through the UI"],"ans":[0],"why":"Auto Loader is designed for continuous or very high volumes of files. It tracks processed files in a checkpoint and can use file notifications instead of listing huge folders. Rereading the whole folder every day duplicates work and data."},{"id":"dbx-012","d":"ingest","q":"An Auto Loader stream reads JSON with schema evolution left at its default. A new field appears in incoming files. What happens by default?","opts":["The new field is silently dropped forever","The stream stops, updates the stored schema with the new column, and picks it up when restarted","Every record with the new field is deleted","The table is recreated from scratch"],"ans":[1],"why":"The default mode, addNewColumns, adds the new column to the schema stored at the schema location and stops the stream so the change is noticed. When it restarts, records include the new column. Jobs are usually set to retry so this happens automatically."},{"id":"dbx-013","d":"ingest","q":"With Auto Loader, where do values go that don't match the expected schema, such as a string in an integer column, when rescue is enabled?","opts":["They are cast to NULL with no trace","Into the _rescued_data column, so nothing is lost","Into a separate error table that must be created first","The whole file is skipped"],"ans":[1],"why":"The rescued data column, _rescued_data by default, keeps values that couldn't be parsed into the schema as JSON, along with the source file path. You can inspect and reprocess them later instead of losing data."},{"id":"dbx-014","d":"ingest","q":"You want Auto Loader to process all files available now and then stop, so it can run as a scheduled batch job. Which trigger do you use?","opts":["trigger(availableNow=True)","trigger(processingTime='0 seconds')","trigger(continuous='1 second')","No trigger, and kill the job after an hour"],"ans":[0],"why":"availableNow processes every file that has arrived since the last run, in as many batches as needed, then stops. It gives incremental, exactly-once file processing on a schedule without paying for an always-on stream.","code":"(spark.readStream.format(\"cloudFiles\")\n  .option(\"cloudFiles.format\", \"json\")\n  .option(\"cloudFiles.schemaLocation\", \"/Volumes/main/raw/_schemas/orders\")\n  .load(\"/Volumes/main/raw/orders/\")\n  .writeStream\n  .option(\"checkpointLocation\", \"/Volumes/main/raw/_checkpoints/orders\")\n  .trigger(????)\n  .toTable(\"main.bronze.orders\"))"},{"id":"dbx-015","d":"ingest","q":"When should Auto Loader use file notification mode instead of directory listing mode?","opts":["When the input folder is small and rarely changes","When the folder receives very large numbers of files and listing it on every run becomes slow and costly","Only when the files are CSV","Never: directory listing is always faster"],"ans":[1],"why":"Directory listing works well for modest folders but has to list storage to find new files. File notification mode (and the newer file events, which the docs prefer) reads storage events, so it scales to huge numbers of files without repeated listing.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/file-events-explained","t":"Auto Loader with file events"}},{"id":"dbx-016","d":"ingest","q":"A company wants to ingest data from Salesforce and SQL Server into Unity Catalog tables with Databricks managing the connection, incremental reads and schema changes. What should they consider first?","opts":["Lakeflow Connect managed connectors","Writing a custom REST client for each source","Exporting CSV files by hand every morning","Auto Loader pointed at the source databases"],"ans":[0],"why":"Lakeflow Connect provides managed connectors for common SaaS applications and databases, handling authentication, incremental ingestion and schema evolution. Auto Loader reads files in cloud storage, not databases or SaaS APIs."},{"id":"dbx-017","d":"ingest","q":"A source system offers only a REST API with no Databricks connector. What is a sensible way to land its data?","opts":["Call the API from a notebook, write the results to a bronze Delta table, and schedule it as a Lakeflow Jobs task","Ask analysts to copy and paste results into a spreadsheet","Point Auto Loader at the API URL","Use COPY INTO with the API URL as the source"],"ans":[0],"why":"When no connector exists, a notebook using a REST or JDBC client can fetch data and write it to a Delta table, orchestrated by Lakeflow Jobs for scheduling and retries. Auto Loader and COPY INTO read files from storage, not APIs. The docs also describe a decoupled pattern: the notebook writes raw JSON to a volume and Auto Loader loads it.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/api-ingestion","t":"Ingest data from an API in pipelines"}},{"id":"dbx-018","d":"transform","q":"A JSON column holds an array of line items per order. You need one row per line item in a silver table. Which function do you use?","opts":["explode","flatten_all","split","pivot"],"ans":[0],"why":"explode turns each element of an array into its own row, repeating the other columns. split breaks a string into an array, and pivot turns row values into columns.","code":"SELECT order_id, item.sku, item.qty\nFROM (\n  SELECT order_id, explode(line_items) AS item\n  FROM main.bronze.orders\n)"},{"id":"dbx-019","d":"ingest","q":"Which statements about loading files into Unity Catalog are true? (Choose two.)","opts":["Volumes give governed access to files in cloud storage through Unity Catalog","COPY INTO can load files from a volume into a table","Files in a volume cannot be read by Spark","Auto Loader can only read files uploaded through the UI"],"ans":[0,1],"why":"Volumes are Unity Catalog objects for files, governed with the same permissions as tables, and both COPY INTO and Auto Loader can read from volume paths such as /Volumes/catalog/schema/volume/. Spark reads volume files like any other path."},{"id":"dbx-117","d":"ingest","q":"COPY INTO loaded a folder yesterday. Today one of yesterday's files was corrected in place and 20 new files arrived. Without any options, what does COPY INTO load?","opts":["Only the 20 new files; the corrected file is skipped because it was already loaded","All files in the folder again","The 20 new files and the corrected file","Nothing, because the folder has already been loaded"],"ans":[0],"why":"COPY INTO is idempotent: it skips files it has already loaded, even when the file was changed afterwards. To reload on purpose, set the force copy option and name the files with FILES or PATTERN, otherwise every matching file is loaded again.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/delta-copy-into","t":"COPY INTO reference"}},{"id":"dbx-118","d":"ingest","q":"An Auto Loader stream has no schema supplied and the default schema evolution mode. Today's files contain a new field. What happens?","opts":["The stream stops once, saves the new column to the schema location, and uses it after a restart","The new field is silently ignored forever","All existing rows are rewritten with the new column immediately","The stream keeps running and the field is dropped"],"ans":[0],"why":"The default with no schema is addNewColumns: the stream fails with an unknown-field error after updating the stored schema, and the restart picks the new column up. Run it under a job with retries. Use rescue to never stop, or failOnNewColumns to demand attention.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/schema","t":"Auto Loader schema inference and evolution"}},{"id":"dbx-119","d":"ingest","q":"Auto Loader is given amount as a DOUBLE, but a record contains the text \"N/A\". Where does the value go?","opts":["Into the _rescued_data column, with the source file path","The stream fails and nothing more loads","It is turned into 0.0","It is written to a hidden error table you must create"],"ans":[0],"why":"Values that do not match the schema are kept in the rescued data column as JSON with the file path, so nothing is lost and the stream keeps running. Query it with a colon path where _rescued_data IS NOT NULL.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/schema","t":"Auto Loader schema inference and evolution"}},{"id":"dbx-120","d":"ingest","q":"Which statements about Lakeflow Connect managed database connectors are true? (Choose two.)","opts":["The ingestion gateway runs continuously on classic compute and is billed while it runs","The ingestion pipeline can run on a schedule on serverless compute","Both the gateway and the pipeline must run on a single-user all-purpose cluster","They read the source tables by running a full copy on every update"],"ans":[0,1],"why":"A managed database connector has a gateway that stays on to capture changes and a pipeline that applies them on a schedule, so the gateway is the cost to watch. It reads change logs rather than copying whole tables each time.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/lakeflow-connect/cdc-overview","t":"Managed database connectors"}},{"id":"dbx-121","d":"ingest","q":"Clickstream JSON has stable event_id and ts fields and a properties object whose keys change weekly. How should it be loaded for fast filtering on event_id and ts?","opts":["Type event_id and ts as columns and keep properties as a VARIANT column","Store the whole record as one VARIANT column and partition by a field inside it","Store everything as one string and parse it on every query","Drop properties, since it changes"],"ans":[0],"why":"Typed columns filter faster, and VARIANT cannot be used as a partition or clustering key. Keeping only the changing part as VARIANT stays flexible. Declare it through cloudFiles.schemaHints.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/variant","t":"Ingest data as VARIANT"}},{"id":"dbx-030","d":"transform","q":"In PySpark, df1.union(df2) is called on two DataFrames with the same columns in a different order. What happens?","opts":["Columns are matched by name automatically","Columns are matched by position, so values can end up in the wrong columns; use unionByName instead","Duplicates are removed like SQL UNION","It always raises an error"],"ans":[1],"why":"DataFrame.union matches columns by position and keeps duplicates, like SQL UNION ALL. If column order differs, values land in the wrong columns. unionByName matches by column name."},{"id":"dbx-031","d":"transform","q":"What is the difference between UNION and UNION ALL in Spark SQL?","opts":["UNION removes duplicate rows; UNION ALL keeps every row and is cheaper","UNION ALL removes duplicates; UNION keeps them","They are identical","UNION ALL only works on streaming tables"],"ans":[0],"why":"UNION de-duplicates the combined result, which needs a shuffle. UNION ALL simply appends, so it is faster. Use UNION ALL unless you really need duplicates removed."},{"id":"dbx-032","d":"transform","q":"A 5 TB orders table is joined to a 20 MB country lookup table. How can you avoid shuffling the large table?","opts":["Broadcast the small table, for example with a broadcast hint","Repartition the orders table to 10,000 partitions","Convert the lookup table to CSV","Use a cross join"],"ans":[0],"why":"A broadcast join sends the small table to every executor, so each partition of the large table joins locally with no shuffle. Spark does this automatically below spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold, and a hint forces it.","code":"SELECT /*+ BROADCAST(c) */ o.*, c.country_name\nFROM main.silver.orders o\nJOIN main.ref.countries c ON o.country_code = c.code"},{"id":"dbx-033","d":"transform","q":"You need to keep one row per order_id from a bronze table where retries created duplicates. Which PySpark call does this?","opts":["df.dropDuplicates([\"order_id\"])","df.distinct(\"order_id\")","df.drop(\"order_id\")","df.groupBy(\"order_id\")"],"ans":[0],"why":"dropDuplicates with a subset keeps one row for each value of the listed columns. distinct takes no column list and compares whole rows, and groupBy needs an aggregation. To choose which duplicate survives, use a window with row_number instead."},{"id":"dbx-034","d":"transform","q":"A dashboard needs the number of distinct users per day over billions of events, and a small error is acceptable. Which function is fastest?","opts":["approx_count_distinct","count(DISTINCT user_id) after collecting to the driver","collect_set then size","countDistinct on a cross join"],"ans":[0],"why":"approx_count_distinct uses the HyperLogLog++ algorithm to estimate distinct counts with a small, configurable error, using far less memory and shuffle than an exact distinct count."},{"id":"dbx-035","d":"transform","q":"When cleaning a bronze table, which PySpark call replaces NULL values in the quantity column with 0?","opts":["df.fillna({\"quantity\": 0})","df.dropna(\"quantity\")","df.replace(None, \"quantity\")","df.na.drop(0)"],"ans":[0],"why":"fillna (also df.na.fill) replaces nulls with a value, here per column through a dictionary. dropna removes rows with nulls instead of filling them."},{"id":"dbx-036","d":"transform","q":"A job joining two large tables writes 200 tiny output files and some tasks process far more data than others. Which setting controls how many partitions Spark uses after a shuffle?","opts":["spark.sql.shuffle.partitions","spark.executor.cores","spark.driver.maxResultSize","spark.sql.files.maxRecordsPerFile"],"ans":[0],"why":"spark.sql.shuffle.partitions sets the number of partitions after joins and aggregations; it defaults to 200. Adaptive query execution can coalesce partitions and split skewed ones at run time, but the setting is still the starting point to tune and re-measure.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/aqe","t":"Adaptive query execution"}},{"id":"dbx-037","d":"transform","q":"Which gold-layer object stores precomputed query results and is refreshed, incrementally where possible, when its sources change?","opts":["A materialized view","A standard view","A temporary view","A volume"],"ans":[0],"why":"A materialized view stores its results and is refreshed from its sources, incrementally when the query allows. A standard view stores no data and runs its query each time it is read.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/incremental-refresh","t":"Incremental refresh for materialized views"}},{"id":"dbx-038","d":"transform","q":"Which object is the best fit for append-only data that should be processed exactly once as it arrives, such as events from Auto Loader?","opts":["A streaming table","A materialized view","A standard view","A temporary view"],"ans":[0],"why":"Streaming tables process each new input row once, which suits append-only sources like files or event streams. Materialized views suit results that may need recomputing when sources change, such as aggregates and joins.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/concepts/streaming-tables","t":"Streaming tables"}},{"id":"dbx-039","d":"transform","q":"In a Lakeflow Declarative Pipeline, a constraint is defined as below. What happens to rows where order_id is NULL?","opts":["They are dropped from the target and counted in the pipeline's data quality metrics","The whole pipeline update fails","They are kept and nothing is recorded","They are moved to a quarantine table automatically"],"ans":[0],"why":"ON VIOLATION DROP ROW removes failing rows and records how many were dropped. Without an ON VIOLATION clause, failing rows are kept and only counted, and ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE stops the update.","code":"CREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE silver_orders (\n  CONSTRAINT valid_id EXPECT (order_id IS NOT NULL) ON VIOLATION DROP ROW\n) AS SELECT * FROM STREAM(bronze_orders)","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/expectations","t":"Manage data quality with pipeline expectations"}},{"id":"dbx-040","d":"transform","q":"Which statements describe a medallion architecture? (Choose two.)","opts":["Bronze holds raw data close to the source, kept for reprocessing","Silver holds cleaned, conformed data such as de-duplicated and typed records","Gold is where raw files first land","Each layer must live in a separate workspace"],"ans":[0,1],"why":"Bronze keeps raw data so you can rebuild later, silver holds cleaned and conformed data, and gold holds business-level aggregates and models for BI and ML. Layers are usually schemas or catalogs, not separate workspaces."},{"id":"dbx-122","d":"transform","q":"On serverless compute, a query runs cast(quantity AS INT) over a bronze column that contains values like \"N/A\". What happens, and what is the fix?","opts":["The query fails because ANSI mode is on; use try_cast, which returns NULL for values that do not fit","Bad values silently become 0; use cast with a default","Bad values are moved to _rescued_data","The query succeeds and bad values stay as text"],"ans":[0],"why":"Serverless runs with spark.sql.ansi.enabled set to true, so an invalid cast raises an error and stops the query. try_cast returns NULL instead, so you keep the row and can count the failures.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/try_cast","t":"try_cast function"}},{"id":"dbx-123","d":"transform","q":"Two DataFrames hold the same orders data, but the second has its columns in a different order and one extra column. Which call combines them correctly?","opts":["df1.unionByName(df2, allowMissingColumns=True)","df1.union(df2)","df1.join(df2, how=\"cross\")","df1.intersect(df2)"],"ans":[0],"why":"union matches columns by position, so a different order puts values in the wrong columns. unionByName matches by name, and allowMissingColumns fills the missing column with NULL on the other side."},{"id":"dbx-124","d":"transform","q":"Orders have an items array that is empty for cancelled orders. You need one row per item, but cancelled orders must still appear. Which function do you use?","opts":["explode_outer","explode","flatten","split"],"ans":[0],"why":"explode drops rows whose array is empty or NULL. explode_outer keeps them, with NULL in the new column."},{"id":"dbx-125","d":"transform","q":"Bronze holds several versions of each order_id with an updated_at column. Which approach keeps exactly the latest version of each order?","opts":["Keep rows where row_number() over PARTITION BY order_id ORDER BY updated_at DESC equals 1","df.dropDuplicates([\"order_id\"])","df.distinct()","GROUP BY order_id with max(updated_at) on its own"],"ans":[0],"why":"dropDuplicates keeps one row per key but does not promise which one. row_number over a window ordered by updated_at picks the latest, and QUALIFY does it in one SQL statement. A plain GROUP BY with max returns the timestamp, not the rest of the row."},{"id":"dbx-126","d":"transform","q":"Which statements about materialized views are true? (Choose two.)","opts":["Incremental refresh needs serverless compute; on classic compute each refresh recomputes fully","A refresh can be scheduled in the definition, for example SCHEDULE EVERY 1 HOURS","A materialized view stores no data and runs its query on every read","A materialized view must be refreshed by hand with OPTIMIZE"],"ans":[0,1],"why":"Materialized views store their result. When the query and sources allow, a refresh on serverless processes only the changes; otherwise it recomputes. The definition can carry a schedule or a trigger on source updates. OPTIMIZE and VACUUM are not supported because maintenance is automatic.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/incremental-refresh","t":"Incremental refresh for materialized views"}},{"id":"dbx-127","d":"transform","q":"In a pipeline, an expectation uses ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE and a bad row arrives. What happens?","opts":["The update stops and the table write rolls back; no quality metrics are recorded for it","The bad row is dropped and counted","The bad row is kept and counted","The bad row is moved to a quarantine table automatically"],"ans":[0],"why":"FAIL UPDATE stops the update, and someone must fix the cause before rerunning. Because the update failed, the Data quality tab has no metrics for it; read the error in the event log. DROP ROW removes and counts, and the default (warn) keeps and counts.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/expectations","t":"Manage data quality with pipeline expectations"}},{"id":"dbx-128","d":"transform","q":"A notebook on a running classic cluster runs spark.conf.set(\"spark.executor.memory\", \"16g\") to fix out-of-memory errors. Why doesn't it help?","opts":["Executor memory is fixed when the cluster starts; change it in the compute configuration and restart","The value must be in megabytes","spark.conf.set only works in SQL","Executor memory can only be changed by AQE"],"ans":[0],"why":"Session settings such as spark.sql.shuffle.partitions can change at run time, but executor and driver memory are set when the executors start, so they belong in the compute configuration. On serverless you cannot set them at all.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/spark/conf","t":"Set Spark configuration properties"}},{"id":"dbx-050","d":"jobs","q":"In a Lakeflow Job, task C depends on tasks A and B. C should run as long as at least one of them succeeded. Which setting achieves this?","opts":["Set C's \"Run if dependencies\" to At least one succeeded","Remove C's dependencies","Add retries to A and B","Schedule C one hour after A and B"],"ans":[0],"why":"The Run if setting on a task controls how its dependencies' outcomes are combined: All succeeded is the default, and options include At least one succeeded, None failed and All done."},{"id":"dbx-051","d":"jobs","q":"A job's third task failed after the first two finished. After fixing the bug, what is the cheapest way to finish the run?","opts":["Repair the run, which reruns only the failed task and the tasks after it","Start a brand-new run of the whole job","Delete the job and recreate it","Run each notebook by hand"],"ans":[0],"why":"Repair run reruns failed tasks and the tasks that depend on them, reusing the successful tasks' results. A new full run would redo work that already succeeded."},{"id":"dbx-052","d":"jobs","q":"A job must run whenever new files land in a cloud storage location, not on a fixed schedule. Which trigger fits?","opts":["A file arrival trigger","A cron schedule every minute","A continuous trigger on a SQL warehouse","Manual runs only"],"ans":[0],"why":"File arrival triggers start a job when new files appear in the monitored location. Polling with a frequent cron schedule wastes runs when no data arrives."},{"id":"dbx-053","d":"jobs","q":"A gold job should run only after a silver table it reads has been updated by another team's pipeline, which finishes at unpredictable times. Which trigger is the best fit?","opts":["A table update trigger on the silver table","A schedule at 6 am in the hope it is done","A file arrival trigger on the job's own notebook","A for each task"],"ans":[0],"why":"Table update triggers start a job when the watched tables change, so the job runs when the data is actually ready. Data-driven triggers avoid both waiting too long and reading stale data."},{"id":"dbx-054","d":"jobs","q":"A job must run the same notebook for each of 30 country codes. Which task type avoids creating 30 separate tasks?","opts":["A for each task that loops over the list of countries","An if/else condition task","A dashboard task","A file arrival trigger"],"ans":[0],"why":"A for each task runs a nested task once per item in a list, passing the item as a parameter, with a configurable concurrency. Condition tasks branch; they don't loop."},{"id":"dbx-055","d":"jobs","q":"A job should send its data to a quarantine step only when a validation task reports errors. Which feature branches the workflow?","opts":["An if/else condition task that checks a value such as a task value","Increasing the number of retries","A cron schedule","Cluster autoscaling"],"ans":[0],"why":"An if/else condition task evaluates an expression, for example a task value set by the validation notebook, and runs the true or false branch accordingly."},{"id":"dbx-056","d":"jobs","q":"An API call in an ingestion task fails occasionally because of short network errors. What is the simplest fix in the job?","opts":["Configure retries on that task, with a delay between attempts","Make every downstream task run if all done","Move the task to the end of the job","Disable the job's schedule"],"ans":[0],"why":"Task-level retries rerun a failed task automatically, optionally with a wait between attempts, which handles transient failures without manual reruns."},{"id":"dbx-057","d":"jobs","q":"Which task types can a single Lakeflow Job orchestrate? (Choose two.)","opts":["A Lakeflow Declarative Pipeline update","A SQL query or SQL file run on a SQL warehouse","A Git commit on someone's laptop","Restarting the workspace"],"ans":[0,1],"why":"Lakeflow Jobs can run notebooks, Python scripts, SQL queries and files, dashboards, pipeline updates and more, with dependencies between them. They don't run actions on personal machines."},{"id":"dbx-135","d":"jobs","q":"A cleanup task must run after its upstream tasks finish, whether they succeeded or failed. Which Run if condition fits?","opts":["All done","All succeeded","None failed","At least one succeeded"],"ans":[0],"why":"All done runs a task once all its dependencies have finished, whatever their result. The default, All succeeded, would mark the cleanup Upstream failed and skip it exactly when something failed.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/run-if","t":"Configure task dependencies"}},{"id":"dbx-136","d":"jobs","q":"An If/else task compares {{tasks.count.values.n}} with 0 using ==. The upstream task stored 0.0, and the false branch ran. Why?","opts":["== and != compare operands as text, so \"0.0\" does not equal \"0\"","Task values cannot be used in If/else tasks","The If/else task always takes the false branch on its first run","Numbers are rounded before comparison"],"ans":[0],"why":"In an If/else condition task, == and != compare as strings, while >, >=, < and <= compare as numbers. Use a numeric operator, or make sure both sides are written the same way.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/tasks/if-else","t":"Add branching logic with the If/else task"}},{"id":"dbx-137","d":"jobs","q":"A vendor uploads files in bursts over several minutes. The job should start once per burst, after the uploads stop. Which file arrival trigger setting does this?","opts":["Wait after last change","Minimum time between triggers","Max concurrent runs","Retry interval"],"ans":[0],"why":"Wait after last change starts a timer that resets with every new file, so the run starts only once arrivals go quiet. Minimum time between triggers only limits how often runs can start.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/file-arrival-triggers","t":"Trigger jobs when new files arrive"}},{"id":"dbx-138","d":"jobs","q":"A job has a table update trigger on two silver tables with Trigger when set to All tables are updated. The tables are updated every night, but the job never runs and no error appears. What is the most likely cause?","opts":["The job's Run as identity lacks SELECT, USE CATALOG or USE SCHEMA on the tables","Table update triggers only work on external tables","The tables must be in the same schema as the job","All tables are updated only fires once a week"],"ans":[0],"why":"The trigger reads the tables' change history as the job's Run as identity. Without SELECT and the USE privileges it fails to evaluate and starts no runs, and the failure is only reported if failure notifications are configured.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/trigger-table-update","t":"Trigger jobs when source tables are updated"}},{"id":"dbx-139","d":"jobs","q":"A task uses dbutils.jobs.taskValues.set to pass a list to a later task. Which statements are true? (Choose two.)","opts":["The value must be JSON-serialisable and at most 48 KiB","A later task can read it with {{tasks.<task_name>.values.<key>}}","Task values can be set from SQL tasks","Task values persist between separate job runs"],"ans":[0,1],"why":"Task values are set from Python notebooks, must be JSON-serialisable and no larger than 48 KiB, and are read in later tasks of the same run with a dynamic value reference such as {{tasks.get_list.values.items}}.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/task-values","t":"Use task values to pass information between tasks"}},{"id":"dbx-070","d":"cicd","q":"Where do you work on a feature branch of a repository inside the Databricks workspace?","opts":["A Git folder","A volume","The workspace's trash","A SQL warehouse"],"ans":[0],"why":"Git folders connect a workspace folder to a remote Git repository so you can create branches, commit, push, pull and work toward pull requests from Databricks."},{"id":"dbx-071","d":"cicd","q":"In a bundle configuration, the catalog name differs between dev and prod. What is the recommended way to handle it?","opts":["Define a variable and set different values per target","Keep two copies of every notebook","Edit the notebook by hand before each deployment","Hard-code prod and ask developers to be careful"],"ans":[0],"why":"Bundles support variables with per-target overrides in the targets section of databricks.yml, so the same code deploys to dev, test and prod with environment-specific settings."},{"id":"dbx-072","d":"cicd","q":"Which CLI command checks a bundle's configuration for errors before anything is deployed?","opts":["databricks bundle validate","databricks bundle destroy","databricks fs rm","databricks clusters delete"],"ans":[0],"why":"bundle validate checks the configuration and reports problems without deploying. A typical CI pipeline runs validate, then deploy to a target, then run tests or the job."},{"id":"dbx-073","d":"cicd","q":"A CI pipeline should deploy a bundle's jobs and pipelines to production after a merge to main. Which command does the deployment?","opts":["databricks bundle deploy -t prod","databricks bundle init","databricks workspace export","databricks jobs list"],"ans":[0],"why":"bundle deploy -t prod deploys the bundle's resources using the prod target's settings. init creates a new bundle from a template, and the others don't deploy anything."},{"id":"dbx-074","d":"cicd","q":"Why do teams package jobs and pipelines as bundles rather than editing them in the UI in each workspace? (Choose two.)","opts":["Definitions live in version control and can be reviewed in pull requests","The same definitions can be deployed repeatably to dev, test and prod","Bundles make jobs run without compute","Bundles remove the need for permissions"],"ans":[0,1],"why":"Bundles describe resources as code, so changes are reviewed and versioned, and one definition deploys consistently to every environment. They don't change how jobs use compute or permissions."},{"id":"dbx-140","d":"cicd","q":"In a Git folder, how do you get your branch reviewed and merged into main?","opts":["Commit & Push the branch, then open a pull request in the Git provider","Click Create pull request in the Git folder dialog","Run Reset to main and push","Rebase main onto your branch in the workspace"],"ans":[0],"why":"Git folders handle branches, Commit & Push, pull, merge and rebase, but pull requests are created and merged in the Git provider such as GitHub or Azure DevOps.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/repos/git-operations-with-repos","t":"Create and manage Git folders"}},{"id":"dbx-141","d":"cicd","q":"A developer runs databricks bundle deploy without -t. The job appears as \"[dev asha] daily_sales\" and its schedule does not fire. Why?","opts":["The default target uses mode: development, which prefixes names per user and pauses schedules","Bundles cannot deploy schedules","The deploy failed and created a placeholder job","Schedules only start after databricks bundle summary"],"ans":[0],"why":"Without -t, the CLI uses the target marked default: true. Development mode prefixes resource names with [dev user], tags them, and pauses schedules and triggers so personal copies do not run on their own.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/deployment-modes","t":"Bundle deployment modes"}},{"id":"dbx-142","d":"cicd","q":"A bundle variable catalog has a default of dev_main, the prod target sets it to main, and the CI system runs databricks bundle deploy -t prod --var=\"catalog=main_v2\". Which value is used?","opts":["main_v2","main","dev_main","The deploy fails because the value is set twice"],"ans":[0],"why":"The --var option has the highest precedence, then BUNDLE_VAR_ environment variables, then the target's variables, then the default. Here --var wins.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/variables","t":"Substitutions and variables"}},{"id":"dbx-143","d":"cicd","q":"The prod target overrides a job cluster with only num_workers: 8, using the same job_cluster_key as the top-level definition. What does the deployed cluster look like?","opts":["The top-level settings, with num_workers changed to 8","Only num_workers is set; every other setting is lost","The top-level cluster is used unchanged","Two job clusters are created"],"ans":[0],"why":"Target resources merge with top-level resources, matched by key (job_cluster_key for job clusters, task_key for tasks). Settings in only one place are kept; where both set a value, the target wins.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/overrides","t":"Override with target settings"}},{"id":"dbx-144","d":"cicd","q":"Production jobs deployed from CI must not run as the person who merged the change. What should the prod target set?","opts":["run_as with the service principal's application ID, and deploy from CI as that service principal","mode: development","default: true","A personal access token of the team lead in databricks.yml"],"ans":[0],"why":"run_as sets the identity jobs run as. Using a service principal for both the CI deploy and run_as keeps production independent of any person, and secrets stay in the CI system, never in the repository.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/run-as","t":"Specify a run identity for a bundle workflow"}},{"id":"dbx-090","d":"tune","q":"In the Spark UI, one stage has 200 tasks: most finish in 10 seconds but three take 15 minutes. What is the most likely cause?","opts":["Data skew: a few keys hold far more rows than the rest","Too much driver memory","The cluster has too few users","Photon is enabled"],"ans":[0],"why":"A large gap between median and maximum task time in one stage is the classic sign of skew. Fixes include adaptive query execution's skew join handling, salting hot keys, or filtering them separately."},{"id":"dbx-091","d":"tune","q":"A stage shows large values for spill (disk). What does this mean?","opts":["Tasks ran out of memory for their data and wrote it to disk, which slows them down","The job wrote its output to a volume","The cluster disk is full of logs","Delta deleted old files"],"ans":[0],"why":"Spill means data didn't fit in execution memory and was written to disk and read back. More partitions (smaller tasks), less skew or more memory per core usually help."},{"id":"dbx-092","d":"tune","q":"Which statements about liquid clustering are true? (Choose two.)","opts":["You can change the clustering keys later without rewriting the existing data","It is defined with CLUSTER BY and replaces Hive-style partitioning and ZORDER for new tables","It requires partitioning the table by date first","It only works on external CSV tables"],"ans":[0,1],"why":"Liquid clustering is set with CLUSTER BY, and keys can be changed with ALTER TABLE as query patterns change; existing data isn't rewritten immediately. It is the recommended layout for new Delta tables instead of partitioning plus ZORDER."},{"id":"dbx-093","d":"tune","q":"What does predictive optimization do for Unity Catalog managed tables?","opts":["Runs maintenance such as OPTIMIZE and VACUUM automatically when it would help","Predicts query results before they run","Moves tables to cheaper storage classes","Rewrites SQL queries to use fewer joins"],"ans":[0],"why":"Predictive optimization decides when maintenance is worthwhile and runs operations like OPTIMIZE and VACUUM for managed tables, so teams don't need to schedule them by hand."},{"id":"dbx-094","d":"tune","q":"A notebook calls df.collect() on a 300 GB DataFrame and the job fails with an out-of-memory error on the driver. What is the best fix?","opts":["Write the result to a table, or aggregate before collecting, instead of collecting all rows to the driver","Add more worker nodes","Increase spark.sql.shuffle.partitions","Restart the cluster and try again"],"ans":[0],"why":"collect brings every row to the driver, which can't hold 300 GB. More workers don't help the driver. Keep the work distributed and write results out, or collect only small aggregates."},{"id":"dbx-145","d":"tune","q":"In a stage's Summary Metrics, the 75th percentile task duration is 10 seconds and the Max is 20 minutes. What does this most likely show?","opts":["Data skew: a few partitions hold far more data than the rest","Disk spill on every task","Too many executors","A cluster start-up failure"],"ans":[0],"why":"A Max far above the 75th percentile means a few tasks do most of the work, usually because a few keys hold most rows. Check AQE's skew join handling, remove junk hot keys, or salt the key.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/spark-ui-guide/long-spark-stage-page","t":"Skew and spill"}},{"id":"dbx-146","d":"tune","q":"Which statements about predictive optimization are true? (Choose two.)","opts":["It works only on Unity Catalog managed tables","It can run OPTIMIZE, VACUUM and ANALYZE automatically","It also maintains external tables if they are Delta","It runs on the job's own cluster"],"ans":[0,1],"why":"Predictive optimization decides when to run OPTIMIZE (including incremental liquid clustering), VACUUM and ANALYZE on Unity Catalog managed tables, using serverless compute. External tables are not covered.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/predictive-optimization","t":"Predictive optimization"}},{"id":"dbx-147","d":"tune","q":"You changed a table's liquid clustering keys with ALTER TABLE ... CLUSTER BY. What reclusters the existing data with the new keys?","opts":["OPTIMIZE table_name FULL","ALTER TABLE ... CLUSTER BY NONE","VACUUM table_name","Nothing; old data is reclustered automatically at the next query"],"ans":[0],"why":"Changing keys does not rewrite data. Plain OPTIMIZE clusters new data incrementally; OPTIMIZE FULL reclusters everything, which can take hours on large tables.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/clustering","t":"Use liquid clustering for tables"}},{"id":"dbx-148","d":"tune","q":"A notebook runs %pip install of a newer version of a package, but the next cell still imports the old version. What is missing?","opts":["dbutils.library.restartPython() after the install","Restarting the whole cluster from the admin console","Installing it again as a cluster library","Running the cell twice"],"ans":[0],"why":"%pip installs into the notebook's environment but does not restart Python, so already-loaded modules stay. Call dbutils.library.restartPython() after installing, ideally at the top of the notebook, and pin versions with ==.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/libraries/notebooks-python-libraries","t":"Notebook-scoped Python libraries"}},{"id":"dbx-149","d":"tune","q":"A job on classic compute fails before any task runs, and the compute event log shows INIT_SCRIPT_FAILURE. What do you do first?","opts":["Read the init script's logs (via cluster log delivery) and fix the script","Increase spark.sql.shuffle.partitions","Add more workers","Enable predictive optimization"],"ans":[0],"why":"The termination code says a cluster-scoped init script failed. Its stderr log, delivered with cluster log delivery, shows the failing command. Spark settings and worker counts cannot help because Spark never started.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/compute/troubleshooting/cluster-error-codes","t":"Classic compute termination error codes"}},{"id":"dbx-110","d":"gov","q":"A Unity Catalog managed table is dropped. What happens to its data files?","opts":["They are deleted, because Unity Catalog manages the table's storage","They stay in place and must be deleted by hand","They move to the user's home folder","Nothing; managed tables cannot be dropped"],"ans":[0],"why":"For managed tables, Unity Catalog owns the storage, so DROP TABLE removes the data as well as the definition (after a short recovery window). Dropping an external table removes only the metadata."},{"id":"dbx-111","d":"gov","q":"A team must keep data files in a storage path that other systems also read, and dropping the table must not delete them. Which table type fits?","opts":["An external table","A managed table","A temporary view","A materialized view"],"ans":[0],"why":"External tables point to data at a location you manage, registered through an external location. Dropping them removes only the table definition, leaving files for other systems."},{"id":"dbx-112","d":"gov","q":"An analyst group has SELECT on main.sales.orders but gets a permission error when querying it. Which privileges are most likely missing?","opts":["USE CATALOG on main and USE SCHEMA on main.sales","MODIFY on the table","CREATE TABLE on the schema","Workspace admin"],"ans":[0],"why":"To read a table you need USE CATALOG on its catalog and USE SCHEMA on its schema, as well as SELECT on the table. Privileges granted on a catalog or schema are inherited by the objects inside it."},{"id":"dbx-113","d":"gov","q":"Which statement grants the data_analysts group read access to every current and future table in the main.sales schema (given they can use the catalog)?","opts":["GRANT USE SCHEMA, SELECT ON SCHEMA main.sales TO `data_analysts`","GRANT SELECT ON TABLE main.sales.* TO `data_analysts`","GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON METASTORE TO `data_analysts`","GRANT READ FILES ON SCHEMA main.sales TO `data_analysts`"],"ans":[0],"why":"Granting SELECT on a schema is inherited by all tables in it, including ones created later, and USE SCHEMA lets the group reach them. Wildcards on table names are not supported, and granting on the metastore gives far too much."},{"id":"dbx-114","d":"gov","q":"Sales reps should see only rows for their own region in a shared table, without separate copies per region. What does Unity Catalog provide for this?","opts":["A row filter: a function applied to the table that returns which rows each user may see","A column mask on the region column","A separate external table per region","Revoking SELECT from everyone"],"ans":[0],"why":"A row filter attaches a SQL function to a table and is evaluated for every query, often checking group membership with is_account_group_member. Column masks hide or transform values in a column instead of removing rows.","code":"CREATE FUNCTION main.sec.region_filter(region STRING)\nRETURN is_account_group_member('admins') OR is_account_group_member(concat('sales_', region));\n\nALTER TABLE main.sales.orders SET ROW FILTER main.sec.region_filter ON (region);"},{"id":"dbx-115","d":"gov","q":"Only the finance group should see full card numbers; everyone else should see only the last four digits. Which feature fits?","opts":["A column mask function on the card number column","A row filter on the table","Dropping the column for everyone","A view per user"],"ans":[0],"why":"A column mask is a function attached to a column that returns the real value or a masked one depending on who queries, for example the last four digits for users outside finance."},{"id":"dbx-116","d":"gov","q":"A security team wants rules like \"mask every column tagged pii for users outside the privacy group\" applied across many tables from one place. Which Unity Catalog capability is designed for this?","opts":["Attribute-based access control (ABAC) policies based on governed tags","Granting SELECT table by table","Copying tables into a restricted workspace","Turning off Unity Catalog for those tables"],"ans":[0],"why":"ABAC policies use tags on data and conditions on users to apply row filters and column masks centrally, at the catalog or schema level, so new tagged tables are covered automatically instead of configuring each table."},{"id":"dbx-150","d":"gov","q":"Which privileges does a user need to create an external table at s3://acme/sales/ in main.sales? (Choose two.)","opts":["CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE on the external location that covers the path","CREATE TABLE on main.sales, with USE CATALOG and USE SCHEMA","MODIFY on the metastore","BROWSE on main"],"ans":[0,1],"why":"An external table needs CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE on the external location covering its LOCATION, plus the usual CREATE TABLE on the schema with USE CATALOG and USE SCHEMA.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/external","t":"Work with external tables"}},{"id":"dbx-151","d":"gov","q":"An external Delta table should become a Unity Catalog managed table without changing its name or grants. Which statement does this?","opts":["ALTER TABLE main.legacy.events SET MANAGED","ALTER TABLE main.legacy.events SET MANAGED MOVE","CONVERT TO DELTA main.legacy.events","DROP TABLE and CREATE TABLE without LOCATION"],"ans":[0],"why":"ALTER TABLE ... SET MANAGED copies the data and log into managed storage with a short write pause, keeping the name, history and grants. It needs table ownership and Runtime 17.3 LTS or serverless. MOVE and COPY are for foreign tables only.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/convert-to-managed","t":"Convert external tables to managed"}},{"id":"dbx-152","d":"gov","q":"Contractors must lose access to one table in a schema where their group has SELECT on the whole schema. Which approach works in Unity Catalog?","opts":["Revoke SELECT on the schema and grant SELECT only on the tables they may read","Run DENY SELECT ON TABLE for the contractors group","Grant contractors MANAGE on the table","Set the table's owner to the contractors group"],"ans":[0],"why":"Unity Catalog has no DENY statement; grants add up and access is removed with REVOKE. Narrow the grant to the allowed tables, or move the sensitive table to a schema the group cannot use.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/manage-privileges","t":"Manage privileges in Unity Catalog"}},{"id":"dbx-153","d":"gov","q":"Who can grant SELECT on main.gold.sales to another group? (Choose two.)","opts":["The table's owner","A user with MANAGE on the table or its schema","Any user with SELECT on the table","Any user with USE SCHEMA on main.gold"],"ans":[0,1],"why":"Owners of the object or its catalog or schema, metastore admins, and holders of MANAGE can grant privileges. Holding SELECT or USE SCHEMA only lets you use the object.","more":{"u":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/access-control/permissions-concepts","t":"Unity Catalog permissions model"}}]}];
